## Codebase Patterns

- Three `AGENTS.md` files now carry the durable contracts: the root one
  (three applications over one library, the two typecheck
  configurations, the test wiring), `example-shared/AGENTS.md` (a
  directory with two consumers, so every edit is two edits), and
  `example-realistic-demo/AGENTS.md` (the longest, and the first thing
  to read before touching that demo). Put a lasting rule there rather
  than only here; this file is a session log, those are read by anyone.
- Evidence has four kinds and they are not equal: a Node test runs on
  every change, a probe or browser check runs when someone runs it, and
  a step recorded in this log verified the behaviour once and cannot
  notice it breaking. Before calling a criterion covered, say which
  kind. `docs/implementation-plans/2026-07-27-realistic-demo/ac-coverage.md`
  is the matrix; add a row when you add a criterion, and check it there
  before assuming something is tested.
- A child component is an *unexpanded vnode* in the node suite. Calling
  a parent as a plain function gives you the child's type and props and
  nothing below it, so a mutation inside the child cannot be killed from
  the parent's test file however thorough it is. Assert the props at the
  boundary in the parent, and the rendering in the child's own test
  file. A prop the child silently ignores is invisible to both
  otherwise -- that is exactly how `HowToUse` ignoring `steps` survived.
- A boolean that drives copy needs two assertions. Put the state in a
  `data-` attribute *and* switch the sentence, then assert both: the
  attribute, and that the two renderings differ. Checking only the
  attribute passes against a page showing the wrong sentence with the
  right mark on it. `JSON.stringify(childrenOf(a)) !==
  JSON.stringify(childrenOf(b))` keeps the rule against asserting on
  HTML text and still kills the mutation.
- Chromium logs every 404 *response* to the console, so a browser check
  asserting "no console errors" fails on a page whose whole job is to
  handle a 404. Do not filter the message blind -- that hides a missing
  asset. Record 404 responses off `page.on('response')`, filter the
  console line, then assert separately that every 404 URL was an
  expected one and that the expected one actually happened.
- A rule that only one party exercises is a rule nothing has tested. For
  the whole of Phase 7 the creator was the only client that ever wrote to
  the log, so the room withholding an entry from its sender looked
  correct; the moment a second client writes, that client's cursor is
  stranded below its own `seq` and every later entry reads as a gap. When
  a phase adds a second writer, sender, or approver to something, re-ask
  the earlier phase's rules with two of them.
- When a mutation survives a *browser* harness, run the same mutation
  against the Node suite before calling the line untested. Two writers of
  the same record make each other's mutants equivalent end to end:
  removing the explicit save from `setPersist(true)` changes nothing
  observable in a browser, because `watch()`'s effect writes the same
  record, but it fails three Node assertions. Equivalent mutant, not a
  weak check -- and the way to tell them apart is which layer can see it.
- Review the words on the page as carefully as the code. This demo
  teaches by asserting things about itself, so a disclosure that is
  false is a defect of the same kind as a wrong epoch -- and it is
  invisible to every test, because copy is not asserted on (the global
  rule forbids testing HTML text). Read each user-facing sentence and
  ask which line of code makes it true. Phase 7's waiting view said the
  tab could be closed and the invitation picked up later; nothing
  persisted, so closing it threw away the only key that could open the
  Welcome.
- Anything a socket sends is two or more independent fields, and the
  room stores each verbatim. It parses no key package and no commit, so
  fields that are supposed to agree only agree if the *client* checks
  they do -- see `keyPackageBelongsTo`. When reviewing a handler, list
  the fields it trusts together and ask who bound them.
- A terminal client state ends the entry stream, but it does not empty
  the queue. When a client stops being able to process entries -- being
  removed is the Phase 7 case -- the guard belongs at the top of
  `applyEntry`, not in the thing that closed the socket. Entries already
  pushed are still drained, and a throw there is read as a fatal commit
  failure that overwrites the page's real explanation.
- When reviewing, read each test for what it declines to assert. A test
  that checks only an absence ("no second socket opened") passes just as
  happily against code that left the page in a wrong state, and that is
  how two Phase 6 defects survived to US-009. Absence and presence both
  need saying, in the same test.
- Browser verification with real, separate profiles needs no new
  dependency: `playwright` is already in `node_modules` (via tapout) with
  Chromium installed, and `chromium.launchPersistentContext(dir)` per
  client gives one profile -- one user data directory -- per client.
  `example-realistic-demo/scripts/verify-phase7.mjs` is the harness; read
  its header before writing another one.
- `context.setOffline(true)` does **not** close a WebSocket that is
  already open. Traffic silently stops, the page still reads
  "Connection: open" and the room still lists the member as connected --
  so every "went away" check passes or hangs for the wrong reason. To
  really drop one: `addInitScript` a `WebSocket` subclass that pushes
  each instance onto `window.__mlsSockets`, then close it from the page.
  Keep `setOffline` as well, or the client reconnects immediately.
- Filter those sockets by `url.includes('/api/room/')`. A Vite dev page
  holds two: the room's and the HMR channel's. Closing the HMR socket
  makes the Vite client reload the page, which throws away the identity,
  group and cursor the check was about -- and it looks exactly like a
  client bug.
- The client's `cursor` is not rendered anywhere. The one place it is
  observable is the `hello` frame, which carries it, so "the cursor was
  adopted verbatim" is checked by forcing a reconnect with no entry in
  between and reading the next `hello`.
- Nothing in the realistic demo persists before Phase 8, so "close the
  tab and reopen it" is a *new client*, not the same one returning. Any
  plan step written that way has to be run against a dropped socket
  instead, and the difference is worth stating in the log rather than
  quietly substituting one for the other.
- eslint here runs `no-useless-return`, so a void function cannot close
  an early-return chain with a trailing `return`. Where the last branch
  would need one, use `else if`; a fall-through comment that describes
  the branch that is actually falling through is the bug this prevents.
- The Room Durable Object's post-expiry state is reachable without
  waiting three days, and without the alarm's socket close getting in the
  way: add a temporary RPC method that does exactly what `alarm()` does to
  storage (`deleteAll`, `deleteAlarm`, `ensureSchema`) and a temporary
  route calling it. Sockets stay attached, which is the state an in-flight
  message arriving just past the alarm sees. Remove both before
  committing and confirm with
  `grep -n "TEMPORARY" example-realistic-demo/index.ts`.
- In `scripts/probe.mjs`, always `await waitForClose(ws)` after
  `ws.close()`. A socket left closing outlives its check, and the room
  still lists it in `getWebSockets()` -- so a later check's Welcome or
  roster can be answered by the previous check's corpse. This has already
  been a source of cross-check interference.
- Authorization rules for the realistic-demo Worker are pure functions in
  `example-realistic-demo/room-logic.ts`, applied by small `requireX`
  helpers in `index.ts` that send the refusal. Keep the rule pure and the
  reply in the helper; see `mayWriteLog`/`requireMember`. That directory
  has its own `AGENTS.md`.
- A probe check for a gate must assert both polarities: who is refused and
  who still gets through. A check that only asserts the refusal passes
  against a gate that refuses everyone. Assert the refusal's effect as
  well as its reply -- "nothing was written" and "an error came back" are
  different facts.
- `example-realistic-demo/tsconfig.json` has an explicit four-file
  `include`, so the Worker typecheck does not cover `client/`. The root
  `tsconfig.json` is what typechecks the client; confirm with
  `npx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit --listFiles | grep client`.
- eslint's `no-void` rejects the `void somePromise()` idiom the phase
  files use for floating promises. Call it plainly instead.
- A test helper that reads `items[i]` should return an empty stand-in
  rather than indexing straight into the array. A short result then
  fails the assertion that names the behaviour, instead of throwing and
  aborting the whole tapzero file -- which hides every later test in it.
  Type that stand-in as a hand-written all-optional interface, not
  `Partial<A & B>`: TypeScript reduces an intersection of two
  discriminated-union members to `never`, so every property read off it
  is an error, and `npm test` will not tell you because esbuild only
  strips types.
- Tree index math (root/left/right/leafWidth/isLeaf/nodeToLeafIndex) lives in
  `src/treemath.ts` -- always reuse it rather than re-deriving indices.
- Tests live under `test/*.ts` and must be wired into `test/index.ts` via a
  side-effect import (registers the tap tests). `npm run test:node` bundles
  from `test/index.ts`.
- Targeted test run for a single file without touching the full suite:
  `npx esbuild test/<file>.ts --bundle --platform=node --format=cjs --loader:.json=json --keep-names --outfile=.tmp.cjs && node .tmp.cjs; rm .tmp.cjs`
- `npm test` runs longer than the ten-minute cap on a foreground shell
  command, so running it in the foreground kills the session's own tool
  call, not the suite. Start it in the background writing to a file
  (`npm test > /tmp/out.txt 2>&1; echo "EXIT=$?" >> /tmp/out.txt`) and do
  the write-up while it runs. The `EXIT=` line is the gate; a tap run
  that ends in failures still prints `# ok`-adjacent output above it.
  Start it as the session's *first* action, not after the review. A
  session that saves the gates for the end runs out of room before the
  commit, which is how US-007 stalled twice.
- Starting the slow gate early is only half of it. Do the cheap durable
  steps -- the log entry, the `passes` flip, `git add` -- *while* it
  runs, so a green gate is one command away from a commit. US-007
  stalled three times because every session ordered it the other way and
  ran out of room in the gap between the gate and the commit.
- Never call `navigator.clipboard.readText()` through the Playwright
  MCP. It blocks on a permission prompt nothing here can answer, and
  the tool call hangs for the full 1800s idle timeout, taking the dev
  servers with it. To check a copy control: wrap
  `navigator.clipboard.writeText` with a pass-through recorder to
  capture the argument, and for a real round trip focus a throwaway
  input and send `ControlOrMeta+v` with `browser_press_key`, which
  needs no permission.
- To measure client timing from the browser without editing the
  client, replace `globalThis.WebSocket` with a subclass that records
  `Date.now()` in its constructor. `delivery-client.ts` constructs its
  socket at connect time, so it picks up the subclass; the gaps
  between marks are the reconnect delays.
- The standing rule's `pkill -f ".../webcrypto-mls/node_modules"` kills
  Vite and esbuild along with the Worker. To stop only the Worker,
  narrow to `.../node_modules/@cloudflare/workerd` and
  `.../node_modules/wrangler`, which are still repo-scoped.
- zsh has no bash `PIPESTATUS`; it uses `$pipestatus[1]`, 1-indexed. So
  `cmd | tail -5; echo $?` reports `tail`'s status, not the command's,
  and a failing gate reads as a passing one. For any gate, redirect and
  check directly: `npm run lint >/tmp/lint.txt 2>&1; echo $?`.
- A promise chain inside a module serialises that module against itself
  and against nothing else. Two modules that both read a signal, await,
  and write it back are a lost update however carefully each one queues
  its own work -- so the ordering has to be shared and passed in, and the
  read has to be *inside* the shared job. See `createGroupLock` and the
  three modules that take it. Look for this shape whenever a phase adds a
  second writer to state an earlier phase only ever wrote from one place.
- Persistence restores the fields in the record; every signal the page
  derives from them at runtime has to be re-derived on the way back in,
  or a reload comes back in a state that never existed. `state.removed`
  was the case here: the removal is in the stored `ClientState`, the
  signal the view reads is not. When reviewing a restore path, list the
  signals the page writes *in response to* the restored data, not just
  the ones the record holds.
- A parameter typed `string|null` where null means two different things
  ("nothing was asked for" and "what was asked for is invalid") hides the
  difference from every caller. Pass the discriminated union that already
  exists -- `storedRoomIsWanted` takes the whole `Route` for this reason.
- For a guard clause that returns a constant, mutate the *constant*, not
  the clause. Deleting `if (asked.kind === 'gone') return false` is an
  equivalent mutant, because the code below happens to answer false too;
  flipping it to `return true` is the mutation with a behavioural
  difference to kill.
- An entry in this log is a claim about the tree, not a record of it.
  Two entries here ended "and committed" with the work still
  uncommitted and `passes` still false. Before trusting a handoff, check
  `git status` and the story's `passes` flag, and re-run whatever gates
  the commit -- mutation kills above all, since a pattern that silently
  matched nothing reads exactly like a kill. Then *finish it*: the
  failure mode when a story is found half-done in the tree is starting
  it again from the top, which is how US-007 and US-021 each stalled
  three times. Verifying real work and doing the durable steps costs a
  fraction of redoing it.
- To test that something waits, the test has to own the window in which
  it is waiting. `deferred()` plus `longEnoughToHaveFinished()` in
  `test/example-realistic-demo/async-helpers.ts`: hold the lock, start
  the thing under test, assert nothing happened *and* nothing was shown
  as though it had, release, assert it then happened in full. Asserting
  only the settled state passes against no ordering at all.
- Mutation testing is worth scripting rather than doing by hand: a small
  node script holding a list of [name, from, to] triples, which copies
  the file aside, applies one replacement, confirms it landed with
  `diff -q`, rebuilds and runs the targeted bundle, then restores by
  file copy. A pattern that silently fails to match reads exactly like a
  killed mutation otherwise. Report a non-matching pattern as SKIP, not
  as a pass.
- That harness must not score kills on the failure count alone. A
  mutation that makes a test *throw* aborts the tap run: no `not ok`
  line, no `# fail` line, a short output and a non-zero exit -- which a
  failure counter reads as a clean pass. Score a kill on any of three
  signals: failures, a non-zero exit code, or an assertion count below
  the baseline. Record the baseline count first.
- A mutation that deletes a signal write is only meaningful if the
  asserted value differs from that signal's initial value. The signals in
  `client/state.ts` start at `false`/`null`/`0`, so a test asserting
  `isCreator === false` passes against a handler that never writes it.
  Assert the transition, or both polarities. Same shape as the probe rule
  above: a check must cover who gets through as well as who is refused.
- A mutation can survive because the line is genuinely redundant rather
  than because the tests are weak. Check which before adding a test:
  in `delivery-client.ts` the `data === 'pong'` early return cannot be
  killed, because `JSON.parse('pong')` throws and the catch returns too.
  It is a documented fast path, not a rule.
- The typecheck command the phase plans give,
  `npx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit --declaration false
  --declarationMap false`, fails with two TS5069 errors about
  `declarationDir` before it checks anything. Use the form below.
- `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json` currently reports one pre-existing,
  unrelated error in `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)` (last touched
  in commit 3734c60). Do not attempt to fix it; just confirm no *new*
  errors were introduced.
- Error types live in `src/mls-error.ts` (ValidationError, InternalError,
  CodecError, etc.) -- validation of untrusted input should throw
  ValidationError, never a raw TypeError.
- Zeroization style precedent: `zeroPathSecrets` in `src/path-secrets.ts`
  and `epochSecret.fill(0)` in `src/key-schedule.ts`.
- State is functional: `ClientState` is rebuilt, never mutated in place;
  node objects are shared across state versions via shallow copies
  (`updateArray` in `src/util/array.ts`), so wiping a Uint8Array in place
  can corrupt older state snapshots. Copy before wiping unless the old
  state is provably discarded.
- `unprotectPublicMessage` (src/message-protection-public.ts) returns
  `content: msg.content` unchanged (same object reference) once the
  signature/membership tag verifies -- PublicMessage payloads are never
  encrypted, so checking a field on the post-unprotect
  `AuthenticatedContent.content` is equivalent to checking the original
  `pm.content` field directly; it just happens after authenticity is
  established rather than before.
- To unit-test a validation check independently of signature verification,
  forge a `FramedContentTBS` whose `context` is the real, current
  `groupContext` (so verification succeeds against the receiver's actual
  state) but whose `content` field under test (e.g. `epoch`) is
  deliberately wrong. Verifiers reconstruct `context` from their own
  state, not from the message, so the signature still verifies and the
  forged field reaches the validation logic being tested. See
  `test/scenario/proposal-epoch-mismatch.ts`.
- indexedDB code in `example/` is testable in Node without any new
  dependency: install a ~50-line fake on `globalThis.indexedDB` that
  keeps a `Map` per database name and fires its callbacks from
  `queueMicrotask`, then uninstall it in a `finally`. See
  `installFakeIndexedDB` in `test/example/member-store.ts`. The IDB
  surface the demos actually use is small -- open (with a first-open
  `onupgradeneeded`), a transaction with put/delete, a `getAll`
  request, and `deleteDatabase`.
- Each demo page that persists owns its own indexedDB database, built
  with `createMemberStore({ dbName })` from
  `example-shared/persistence-storage.ts`. There are no unbound storage
  functions to import: `loadAllMembers` returns the entire store and
  the restore path deletes every record it cannot classify, so two
  pages sharing one database would delete each other's members. The
  record key, `memberKey(groupIdB64, name)`, is scoped to whatever the
  page calls a client -- on the multi-device page that is a client id,
  so one record per device falls out with no schema change.

- `example/tree-diagram.ts` is shared by all three demo pages. Give a
  page its own presentation by adding an *optional* prop whose default
  reproduces the existing behavior (see `leafLabels`), or by scoping CSS
  under a wrapper class only that page renders (see `.tree-scroll`).
  Never branch on which page is rendering.

- The storage-persistence block (status label, request button, result
  message) is `StoragePanel` in `example-shared/storage-panel.ts`,
  shared by the persistence and multi-device pages, along with
  `PERSIST_MESSAGES` and `persistOutcome`. Persistence is granted per
  *origin*, so both pages always show the same status; keep the wording
  in one place. `example-shared/storage-persistence.ts` holds only the
  browser API calls.

- The main demo's message CSS (`.message-columns`, `.message-box`,
  `.ciphertext-section`, `.ciphertext`, `.decrypted-text`,
  `.decrypt-btn`) is unscoped, so any page reusing those class names
  gets the look for free. Page-specific overrides belong under that
  page's card selector (`.card.message-history.multi-device`), never on
  the shared selectors.

- Demo actions in `example/demo-actions.ts` report both success and
  failure through the shared `state.status` signal. A page that wraps one
  to write a friendlier status line must check the action's return value
  first, or every failure reads as a success. Some actions -- `rotateKeys`
  -- return `void` and so offer no return value to check; compare the
  acting client's own `groupContext.epoch` before and after instead, since
  only a commit that actually landed advances it. `removeUserFromGroup`
  (singular) is one of these; `removeUsersFromGroup` and
  `addUsersToGroup` do return a boolean. `sendMessage` is `void` too,
  and the epoch trick does not apply to it -- an application message
  never advances the epoch -- so compare `state.messages.value.length`
  before and after instead.
- A client removed from the group keeps its *consumed* key package, and
  MLS forbids adding a key package twice, so anything that removes a
  client and expects it to be addable again has to regenerate the key
  package (and its signature keypair) afterwards. See `removeDevice` in
  `example/multi-device-demo.ts`.
- Driving a demo page from Playwright: click one button, then poll until
  its in-flight label ('Creating...', 'Committing...') disappears before
  clicking the next. Firing several actions at once races on
  `users.value` -- each is a read-modify-write of the same signal. Some
  buttons ('Rotate keys', 'Persist') stay in the DOM and only swap their
  label, so poll on a page value that changes instead (the `.epoch`
  line) rather than on the button vanishing.
- Demo page modules use top-level `await` and `indexedDB`, so nothing
  defined in one is importable from a Node test. To make page logic
  testable, extract it into its own module that takes its I/O as a
  parameter -- see `syncPersistedDevices` in `example/device-sync.ts`,
  which accepts the `saveMember`/`deleteMember` pair rather than
  reaching for a store. The page module then holds only the wiring.
- EXAMPLE_USERS are Alice, Bob, Carl, Desmond, Eloise, Fran, Glorg.

- Vnode walker helpers for those tests live in `test/example/vnode.ts`
  (`allNodes`, `childrenOf`, `findByType`, `findByClass`); it exports no
  tests of its own, so it is not registered in `test/index.ts`. A
  component called directly only yields its own markup -- a child
  component is an unexpanded vnode whose `type` is the child function,
  so assert on the props handed to it (needs
  `(node.type as unknown) === Child` to satisfy tsc).

- Preact components in `example/` are unit testable in the node suite
  with no DOM and no new dependency: call the component function
  directly (`CardHeader(props, {})`) and walk the returned vnode tree
  (`node.type`, `node.props`, `node.props.children`; htm nests children
  in arrays, so flatten while walking). Assert on structure and props
  (class, aria-label, onClick), never on rendered text. Type the walker
  with `unknown` -- a recursive `type Child = VNode | ... | Child[]`
  plus `.flat(Infinity)` makes tsc report TS2589.

- `example-shared/persistence-storage.ts` now holds two store factories
  (`createMemberStore`, `createSessionStore`) sharing one module-scope
  `openDb(dbName)`, so one upgrade path creates both object stores. The
  fake `indexedDB` in `test/example/member-store.ts` is what that path
  runs against in node: any IDB surface the module starts using has to
  be added to the fake, or every test in *that* file dies with a
  TypeError inside `onupgradeneeded` -- which reads like a bug in the
  fake's callers rather than a missing method. It models
  `objectStoreNames.contains` and `createObjectStore(name)` now, and
  takes an optional `stores` map so a test can read what was created.

- tapzero serializes the actual value into the diagnostic when an
  assertion fails, so `t.equal(x, undefined)` where `x` may be a
  `ClientState` throws `Do not know how to serialize a BigInt` (the
  epoch) and aborts the whole tap file instead of failing one test. For
  any assertion whose failure would print MLS state, compare a boolean:
  `t.equal(x === undefined, true, ...)`.

## Run: ralph/audit-fixes-2026-07-22

Source: 2026-07-22 correctness and security audit (RFC 9420). Previous run
(ralph/group-tree-diagram) archived to
specs/archive/2026-07-22-group-tree-diagram/.

## 2026-07-22 - US-001

- Stopped `decodeProposal` (src/proposal.ts) from silently degrading a
  malformed standard proposal body into a numeric custom proposal. It
  previously used `orDecoder(triedStandardDecoder, customFallback)`, so any
  failure inside the standard-type branch (e.g. a truncated body) fell
  through to `decodeProposalCustom`, which just reads the remaining bytes as
  opaque `proposalData` -- silently accepting corrupt input instead of
  failing.
- New implementation: a single `flatMapDecoder(decodeUint16, ...)` reads the
  numeric proposal type once, maps it to a known name via
  `enumNumberToKey(defaultProposalTypes)`, and switches on that name. Known
  names dispatch to their real decoder (whose failure now propagates as
  `undefined`, not silently reinterpreted); the `default` case (name is
  `undefined`, i.e. a genuinely unrecognized numeric type) is the only path
  that reaches `decodeProposalCustom`.
- Files changed: `src/proposal.ts`, `test/codec/proposal.ts`.
- Added two tests in `test/codec/proposal.ts`:
  - a remove proposal (type 3) with its body truncated by one byte now
    decodes to `undefined` instead of a custom proposal.
  - a genuinely unknown type (999) still roundtrips as a custom proposal.
- Verified `test/grease.ts` and `test/scenario/grease.ts` (GREASE numeric
  proposal types) still pass unchanged.

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- `flatMapDecoder`/`flatMapDecoderAndMap` (src/codec/tls-decoder.ts) do not
  fall back on failure -- if the inner decoder returned by the mapping
  function fails, the whole decoder returns `undefined`. `orDecoder` is the
  one that tries a fallback, and it's easy to accidentally use it where a
  strict discriminated dispatch (switch on a decoded tag) is what's wanted.
  When decoding a tagged/discriminated union by a leading enum value, prefer
  `flatMapDecoder(decodeTag, (tag) => decoderForTag(tag))` over
  `orDecoder(tryAllVariants, fallback)`.
- `enumNumberToKey` (src/util/enum-helpers.ts) is the existing helper for
  mapping a raw numeric wire value back to a known string enum key,
  returning `undefined` for unrecognized values -- reuse it instead of
  hand-rolling a switch/lookup over numeric values.
- `applyProposals` (src/client-state.ts) is only ever called on the commit
  path (from `create-commit.ts` and `process-messages.ts`'s `processCommit`),
  never for standalone proposal receipt (`processProposal` in
  process-messages.ts just stores into `unappliedProposals`). So gating
  logic added inside `applyProposals` only affects committed proposals,
  which is exactly where RFC 9420 SS12.1's "MUST reject unknown proposal
  type in a commit" applies -- standalone custom proposals still flow
  through untouched.
---

## 2026-07-22 - US-002

- `applyProposals` in `src/client-state.ts` previously grouped proposals by
  type but silently `return acc` (dropped) any proposal with a numeric
  (custom) `proposalType` before grouping -- so a commit referencing an
  uninterpretable custom proposal type would just silently ignore it
  instead of rejecting the commit, per RFC 9420 SS12.1's MUST-reject rule.
- Added `supportedCustomProposalTypes:number[]` to `ClientConfig`
  (src/client-config.ts, default `[]`) as the opt-in mechanism: an
  application that understands a given custom proposal type lists it there.
  Opted-in custom proposals are still excluded from tree-mutation grouping
  (`grouped` in applyProposals) -- the application is responsible for
  interpreting `proposalData` itself; this config only gates whether the
  *commit* is accepted at all.
- In `applyProposals`, before grouping, added a check that throws
  `ValidationError` if any proposal being applied has a numeric
  `proposalType` not present in `state.clientConfig.supportedCustomProposalTypes`.
- Files changed: `src/client-state.ts`, `src/client-config.ts`,
  `test/scenario/custom-proposal.ts` (updated to opt in via clientConfig
