## 2026-07-23 - US-004

- `applyUpdatePathSecret` (`src/create-commit.ts`, called from both the
  sender's own commit path -- indirectly via test-vectors -- and the
  receiver's `applyTreeUpdate` in `src/process-messages.ts`) used two
  non-null assertions when locating the ciphertext meant for the
  receiver: `updateNode!.encryptedPathSecret[i]!`. A malicious committer
  can send an `UpdatePath` whose `nodes[n]` is missing entirely, or whose
  `encryptedPathSecret` array is shorter than the copath resolution at
  that ancestor requires, and both assertions silently coerce `undefined`
  into a value, so the following `decryptWithLabel(..., ct.kemOutput,
  ct.ciphertext, ...)` throws a raw `TypeError` reading properties off
  `undefined` instead of a clear `ValidationError` (RFC 9420 SS7.6 MUST).
- Fix: right after `firstMatchAncestor` returns, throw `ValidationError`
  if `updateNode === undefined`, then compute
  `filteredResolution = filterNewLeaves(resolution, excludeNodes)` once
  and throw `ValidationError` if
  `updateNode.encryptedPathSecret.length !== filteredResolution.length`
  before ever indexing into either array. The subsequent loop iterates
  `filteredResolution.entries()` (same array, now named) and indexes
  `updateNode.encryptedPathSecret[i]` without a `!` -- safe because the
  length check above already guarantees `i` is in bounds.
- Files changed: `src/create-commit.ts`, `test/index.ts` (wired new test
  file), `test/scenario/update-path-ciphertext-count.ts` (new).
- The acceptance criteria's "add a unit test: processing a commit whose
  UpdatePathNode has fewer ciphertexts...throws ValidationError" required
  a genuine end-to-end commit, not a call directly into
  `applyUpdatePathSecret`, because the interesting failure mode is a
  malicious *peer's* wire message, and forging one needs a validly signed
  `PublicMessage`. The test: builds a real 4-leaf group (alice + bob +
  charlie + dave, alice creates, bob joins), has alice create a genuine
  `wireAsPublicMessage: true` empty-proposal commit (which still carries
  an `UpdatePath` -- see US-001's leaf-width test), then structurally
  clones `commit.publicMessage.content` (already a decoded JS object, not
  wire bytes -- no need to touch the codec) with the first
  `UpdatePathNode` that has any ciphertexts truncated by one entry. Since
  that node is nearest to alice's leaf in the direct path (index 0 in the
  `nodes` array, which is built leaf-to-root), and bob is alice's
  immediate sibling, this is exactly the node bob's private key overlaps
  -- no need to manually recompute `firstMatchAncestor` to pick the right
  node. Then re-signs the tampered content via the exported
  `createContentCommitSignature` (in `src/framed-content.ts`, not
  `create-commit.ts` despite `create-commit.ts` importing it from there)
  using alice's real signature private key and the *pre-commit*
  `groupContext`/`membershipKey` (matching what the original signing used),
  and recomputes the membership tag via the exported `protectPublicMessage`
  (`src/message-protection-public.ts`). This produces a message that
  passes all authenticity checks (signature, membership tag) and reaches
  `applyTreeUpdate` -- which in `processCommit`
  (`src/process-messages.ts`) runs *before* the confirmation-tag check --
  so the target `ValidationError` is the first thing that can fire.
- Verified the test is non-tautological: stashed the `src/create-commit.ts`
  fix and re-ran the test file directly -- every ciphersuite's "throws
  ValidationError" assertion failed pre-fix (the old code threw some
  other error, or none, but never the expected `ValidationError`), then
  restored the fix and re-ran to confirm all pass.
- Ran the targeted test directly (esbuild+node per the pattern above):
  38/38 assertions pass across all 19 ciphersuites. Ran `npm run lint`
  (one `prefer-const` fixup needed on `bobGroup`, then clean) and
  `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json` (two new errors surfaced and were
  fixed -- see Learnings below -- leaving only the pre-existing unrelated
  `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)` error). Ran the full suite
  (`npm test`): 44339 assertions, all pass (up from 44301 in the US-003
  run, i.e. +38, matching the new test's assertion count).

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- `AuthenticatedContentCommit` is exported from `src/authenticated-content.ts`,
  not `src/framed-content.ts` -- easy to guess wrong since
  `create-commit.ts` imports `createContentCommitSignature` from
  `framed-content.ts` in the same import cluster; check each type's actual
  defining module rather than assuming co-located imports share a source.
- `PublicMessage.auth` is typed `FramedContentAuthData`, a union of the
  proposal/application and commit auth-data shapes -- narrow with
  `auth.contentType !== 'commit'` before accessing `confirmationTag`, the
  same discriminated-union pattern already used for `FramedContent` itself
  elsewhere in this codebase.
- When forging a tampered-but-validly-signed protocol message for a test
  (extending the technique from US-003's proposal-epoch-mismatch test to
  commits): `createCommit(..., { wireAsPublicMessage: true })` returns a
  message whose `publicMessage.content` is already a structured JS object
  (not encoded wire bytes), so tampering is a plain object
  spread/`.map()`, no codec round-trip needed. Re-signing needs the
  *pre-commit* `groupContext` and `membershipKey` (the committer's own
  state before `createCommit` was called), since those are what the
  original signature/membership-tag were computed over.
- This codebase's `UpdatePath.nodes` array is ordered leaf-to-root
  (nearest-ancestor first), matching `filteredDirectPathAndCopathResolution`'s
  iteration order -- useful for picking a specific node index to target
  in a test without needing to call `firstMatchAncestor` directly.
