## 2026-07-23 - US-005

- `stripHandshakeRatchets` (src/secret-tree.ts) was mutating the input
  tree's `Uint8Array`s in place (`.fill(0)`) despite its doc comment
  claiming to return a copy. Both call sites (`addHistoricalReceiverData`
  in src/client-state.ts, used from create-commit.ts and
  process-messages.ts) call it on the old `state` while building a new
  `ClientState` -- the old state is still the caller's live reference at
  that point (functional state, not provably discarded), so zeroizing in
  place corrupted a `ClientState` the application might still be holding.
- Fix: `stripHandshakeRatchets` now only builds new node objects with an
  empty handshake secret; it never touches the original buffers. Added a
  new `zeroHandshakeRatchets(tree):void` export for callers that can
  prove a tree is no longer referenced by any live `ClientState` and want
  to reclaim the forward-secrecy benefit explicitly -- not called from
  anywhere yet, since neither existing call site can make that proof.
- Files changed: `src/secret-tree.ts`,
  `test/validation/key-material-retention.ts`.
- Updated the existing test (historical receiver data strips handshake
  ratchets but keeps application usable) to assert the original buffer
  is untouched (via a pre-copy + deepEqual) instead of asserting it was
  zeroized.
- Added a new test (addHistoricalReceiverData does not corrupt the
  previous ClientState secretTree) that runs a real `createCommit` and a
  direct `addHistoricalReceiverData` call, then checks the pre-commit
  `ClientState.secretTree` handshake secret is byte-identical afterward.
- Ran only `test/validation/key-material-retention.ts` while iterating,
  then the full `npm test` (44396 assertions) and `npm run lint` as the
  final gate -- both clean. `npx tsc --noEmit` shows only the
  pre-existing unrelated error in test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts.

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- When a "returns a copy" doc comment and the implementation disagree,
  check every call site for whether the input is still live before
  trusting the doc comment -- commit builders in this codebase read from
  the pre-commit `state` well after they've started assembling
  `newState`, so anything passed `state` (or a piece of it) during that
  window must not be mutated in place.
- Precedent for pairing a copy-safe primary function with a separate
  explicit wipe function used only when the caller can prove no other
  reference survives: `stripHandshakeRatchets` / `zeroHandshakeRatchets`
  in src/secret-tree.ts, alongside the pre-existing `zeroPathSecrets` in
  src/path-secrets.ts (which is called only after every reader, including
  createWelcome, has consumed the secrets -- see create-commit.ts's
  comment above its `zeroPathSecretsArray` call).
