## 2026-07-23 - US-006

- The `required_capabilities` support check in `validateProposals`
  (`src/client-state.ts`, was near line 274) evaluated
  `capabiltiesAreSupported` against every leaf in the *pre-mutation*
  `tree`, unlike the neighboring general extension-support check
  (lines 257-263) which already excluded leaves targeted by a `remove`
  proposal in the same commit. So a commit that both removed the one
  member lacking a newly required capability and added the
  `required_capabilities` extension in the same commit was wrongly
  rejected, even though no member would actually lack the capability
  once the commit's mutations were applied.
- Fix: extracted a new `postMutationLeafCapabilities(tree, p):Capabilities[]`
  helper that walks `tree`, skips any leaf targeted by a `remove`
  proposal in `p`, and substitutes the new capabilities from a matching
  `update` proposal (keyed by `senderLeafIndex`, since MLS update
  proposals carry the sender's own new leaf node) in place of the
  leaf's current (pre-commit) capabilities. Both the general
  extension-support check (previously lines 257-263) and the
  `required_capabilities`-specific check (previously lines 274-278) now
  call this helper instead of iterating `tree` directly, so they see
  the same post-mutation membership and their logic no longer needs to
  duplicate the remove-exclusion condition.
- Files changed: `src/client-state.ts`,
  `test/scenario/required-capabilites.ts`.
- Added a new test in `test/scenario/required-capabilites.ts`
  (`requiredCapabilitiesWithRemovalTest`, one run per ciphersuite): alice
  creates a group with no `required_capabilities` extension, adds bob
  (full capabilities) and charlie (minimal capabilities) in one commit,
  then alice commits a `remove` proposal for charlie together with a
  `group_context_extensions` proposal adding `required_capabilities`
  that charlie's capabilities don't satisfy -- asserts the commit is
  accepted (previously it threw `ValidationError`).
- Verified the test is non-tautological: `git stash push -- src/client-state.ts`,
  reran the targeted test directly (esbuild+node per the codebase
  pattern) -- the new assertion failed with
  `ValidationError: Not all members support required capabilities` on
  the very first ciphersuite, confirming it exercises the bug. Restored
  the fix (`git stash pop`) and reran: 57/57 assertions pass across all
  19 ciphersuites (38 pre-existing + 19 new, one per ciphersuite).
- Ran `npm run lint` (clean) and `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`
  (only the pre-existing unrelated `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)`
  error). Ran the full suite (`npm test`): 44415 assertions, all pass
  (up from 44396 in the US-005 run, i.e. +19, matching the new test's
  per-ciphersuite assertion count).

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- MLS `ProposalUpdate.update.leafNode` (`src/proposal.ts`'s `Update`
  interface) is the *new* leaf node the sender is replacing their own
  leaf with -- combined with `senderLeafIndex` (the `Proposals` record's
  per-category sender index, `src/client-state.ts`'s `Proposals`
  interface), this is the pattern for computing a commit's post-mutation
  membership/capabilities without needing to actually apply the tree
  mutation: for each remaining leaf, look up whether an `update`
  proposal targets it and substitute that proposal's `leafNode` data (or
  just `capabilities`, as here) in place of the tree's current leaf data.
- When two neighboring validation checks (like lines 257-263 and 274-278
  here) implement the same "exclude removed leaves" logic slightly
  differently, extracting a shared helper both reduces duplication and
  is exactly the kind of fix that closes gaps where the second copy
  quietly drifted out of sync with the first (see acceptance criteria's
  explicit instruction to reuse/unify the neighboring pattern).
