## 2026-07-23 - US-007

- `processCommit` in `src/process-messages.ts` validated the committer's
  update-path leaf node (`content.content.commit.path.leafNode`) against
  `state.groupContext` -- the pre-commit group context -- even though the
  same commit's GroupContextExtensions proposal(s) may introduce a new
  `required_capabilities` extension. A committer whose own freshly signed
  leaf doesn't support the extension it is introducing was accepted by
  receivers, since the check ran against a context that didn't have the
  new extension yet.
- Fix: moved the `groupContextWithExtensions` computation (previously
  built just before `applyTreeUpdate`, after the leaf-node validation
  block) up above the `content.content.commit.path !== undefined` check,
  and passed it into `validateLeafNodeUpdateOrCommit` instead of
  `state.groupContext`. No other call sites of this variable changed;
  it's still the same value, just computed earlier.
- Files changed: `src/process-messages.ts`,
  `test/scenario/gce-committer-leaf-validation.ts` (new),
  `test/index.ts` (wired new test file).
- Added a test where Alice (committer) and Bob both have pre-commit
  capabilities supporting extension 8, so the `applyProposals`-level
  "everyone remaining supports the new required_capabilities" check
  (which reads the pre-commit tree, not the wire leaf node) passes
  honestly. The test then tampers with the *actual* signed committer
  leaf node in the commit message -- re-signing it with reduced
  capabilities via `signLeafNodeCommit` -- and re-signs the outer
  `FramedContent` via `createContentCommitSignature`, then re-wraps it
  as a `PublicMessage` via `protectPublicMessage` (recomputing the
  membership tag). This simulates a real signed message from a
  buggy/malicious committer whose own new leaf silently fails the
  requirement it's introducing. Verified the test fails (throws nothing,
  `t.ok` assertion fails) on the pre-fix code and passes on the fix by
  stashing/unstashing `src/process-messages.ts`.
- Ran `npm test` (44434 assertions, up from 44422 -- the naturally
  skipped ciphersuites still emit passing `t.comment` calls, so the count
  reflects only the ciphersuites that actually ran the new scenario) and
  `npm run lint`; both clean. `npx tsc --noEmit` shows only the
  pre-existing unrelated `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)` error.

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- Two DIFFERENT places check `required_capabilities` support for the
  committer's own leaf, and they use different data sources: (1) the
  `applyProposals` -> `validateProposals` path
  (`src/client-state.ts` `postMutationLeafCapabilities`) checks the
  *tree's* pre-commit leaf capabilities (or an Update proposal's
  capabilities, if present) -- it never sees the actual signed
  update-path leaf node the committer produced; (2) the separate
  `validateLeafNodeUpdateOrCommit` call in `processCommit`
  (`src/process-messages.ts`) checks the *actual signed wire leaf node*
  from `commit.path.leafNode`. Since `createUpdatePath`
  (`src/update-path.ts`) copies capabilities verbatim from the
  pre-commit tree leaf when generating the new leaf, these two checks
  agree for any honestly-generated commit -- so a test that only calls
  `createCommit` normally cannot exercise a divergence between them. To
  test the wire-leaf-specific check, the message has to be built/tampered
  by hand: extract the honestly-generated commit's `path`, swap in a
  re-signed leaf node with different capabilities
  (`signLeafNodeCommit`, exported from `src/leaf-node.ts`), rebuild the
  outer `FramedContent`/signature with `createContentCommitSignature`
  (exported from `src/framed-content.ts`, using the *same* `proposals`
  array from the original commit -- `Commit.proposals` is
  `ProposalOrRef[]`, not raw `Proposal[]`, so reuse the original rather
  than reconstructing it), and re-wrap as a `PublicMessage` via
  `protectPublicMessage` (`src/message-protection-public.ts`, recomputes
  the membership tag). The original (stale) `confirmationTag` can be
  reused unmodified in the tampered message, since
  `validateLeafNodeUpdateOrCommit` runs well before confirmation-tag
  verification in `processCommit` -- the test never reaches that check.
- `createContentCommitSignature`, `createConfirmationTag`,
  `signLeafNodeCommit`, `applyUpdatePath`, and `protectPublicMessage` are
  all exported low-level primitives that mirror exactly what
  `createCommit` uses internally -- reuse them instead of hand-encoding
  wire structures when a scenario test needs to forge a specific,
  otherwise-valid-looking commit message.
