## 2026-07-23 - US-008

- Fixed the "extensions.length > 0" gate in `src/create-commit.ts` and
  `src/process-messages.ts` that conflated "no GroupContextExtensions
  proposal in this commit" with "the GCE proposal's extension list happens
  to be empty" -- both cases previously fell through to keeping the
  pre-commit `state.groupContext.extensions`, so a commit that
  legitimately cleared all group extensions via an empty GCE proposal was
  silently ignored instead of applied.
- Added `hasGroupContextExtensionsProposal:boolean` to the `memberCommit`
  variant of `ApplyProposalsData` (`src/client-state.ts`), set from
  `grouped.group_context_extensions.length > 0` (i.e. "was a GCE proposal
  present", independent of how many extensions it carries). Both
  consumption sites now branch on this flag instead of
  `extensions.length > 0`.
- Writing the round-trip test (committer commits an empty GCE, receiver
  processes it) exposed a second, previously-latent bug: `createCommit`
  called `createUpdatePath` with the pre-commit `state.groupContext` as
  the HPKE encryption context for update-path node ciphertexts, while the
  receiver's `applyTreeUpdate` decrypts using `groupContextWithExtensions`
  (the post-commit context). Whenever a commit actually changes
  extensions, this made the sender's AAD/context diverge from the
  receiver's, so decryption failed outright (raw `CryptoError`, not a
  `ValidationError`). Fixed by computing `groupContextWithExtensions`
  before calling `createUpdatePath` and passing that in, matching what
  the receiver derives. This bug was not previously caught by any
  existing test because no prior test drove a GCE-extension-changing
  commit through actual receiver-side decryption -- `gce-committer-leaf-validation.ts`
  and `required-capabilites.ts` only checked commit-creation/rejection
  semantics, not full sender-to-receiver update-path round trips.
- Files changed: `src/client-state.ts`, `src/create-commit.ts`,
  `src/process-messages.ts`, `test/scenario/gce-empty-extensions.ts` (new),
  `test/index.ts` (wired new test file).
- Added `test/scenario/gce-empty-extensions.ts`: two-member group where
  alice creates the group with one `application_id` extension, adds bob,
  then commits an empty GCE proposal; asserts both alice's and bob's
  post-commit `groupContext.extensions` are empty and their
  `keySchedule.epochAuthenticator` values match.

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- When a commit changes `GroupContext.extensions` via a GCE proposal, the
  HPKE encryption context for the update path (`createUpdatePath` in
  `src/update-path.ts`, called from `create-commit.ts`) and the
  corresponding decryption context (`applyTreeUpdate`/`updatePrivateKeyPath`
  in `src/process-messages.ts`) must both be built from the *same*
  post-GCE-proposal `GroupContext` (extensions applied, but epoch/treeHash
  not yet bumped -- that happens inside `createUpdatePath`/`applyUpdatePath`
  themselves). Passing the pre-commit context on one side and the
  post-commit context on the other silently breaks HPKE decryption with a
  raw `CryptoError`, not a `ValidationError` -- a good signal to check this
  invariant if a GCE-related change starts throwing HPKE `OpenError`s in a
  round-trip test.
- `application_id` is a good "neutral" extension type to use in tests that
  need *some* group extension without triggering the
  `extensionsSupportedByCapabilities` check: it's a default extension type
  (`src/default-extension-type.ts`), and `extensionsSupportedByCapabilities`
  (`src/extension.ts`) filters out all default extension types before
  checking capabilities, so it's exempt regardless of member capabilities.
