## 2026-07-23 - US-011

- `joinGroup` (src/client-state.ts) previously left the caller's
  `privateKeys.initPrivateKey` intact after a successful Welcome join. RFC
  9420 SS16.8 requires the init key never be reused for a second Welcome; an
  attacker who later recovers process memory could use a lingering init key
  to decrypt the Welcome that admitted this member.
- Fix: `privateKeys.initPrivateKey.fill(0)` is called as the last step of
  `joinGroup`, immediately after `createSecretTree` (the last fallible
  operation) and before the function builds/returns the new `ClientState`.
  Every validation and decryption step in `joinGroup` runs before this line,
  so a thrown `ValidationError`/`CodecError`/`CryptoVerificationError` always
  leaves the key intact for retry; only a genuinely successful join reaches
  the zeroize call.
- Added doc comments: on `joinGroup` itself, and on the
  `initPrivateKey` field of `PrivateKeyPackage` (src/key-package.ts), both
  stating the key is consumed by a successful join and must not be reused
  for another Welcome.
- Files changed: `src/client-state.ts`, `src/key-package.ts`, `test/index.ts`
  (wired new test file), `test/scenario/join-group-init-key-zeroization.ts`
  (new).
- Added `test/scenario/join-group-init-key-zeroization.ts`, run against every
  ciphersuite (mirrors the existing pattern of looping
  `Object.keys(ciphersuites)` and skipping `NotSupportedError`/
  `DependencyError` for environment-unsupported suites like X448/Ed448):
  - after a successful `joinGroup`, the caller's `initPrivateKey` buffer is
    all zeros (with a sanity check that it was non-zero beforehand).
  - after a `joinGroup` that fails (a key package with no matching
    recipient in the Welcome, so `decryptGroupSecrets` throws before any
    later step), the caller's `initPrivateKey` buffer is byte-for-byte
    unchanged.
- Verified targeted test (76 assertions across all ciphersuites), `npm run
  lint` (clean), `npx tsc --noEmit` (only the pre-existing unrelated
  `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)` error), and full `npm test`
  (44646 assertions, all passing).

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- `joinGroup`'s early-exit validation checks (credential, group info
  signature, cipher suite match, ratchet tree validation, confirmation tag)
  are all `throw`-based and run strictly before any state mutation, so
  placing a zeroize/consume side effect right before the `return` at the end
  of the function is sufficient to guarantee "only on success" semantics --
  no need for a try/finally or explicit success flag.
- To simulate a `joinGroup` failure for a zeroization/rollback test without
  constructing a malformed message by hand, generate a key package for a
  third party (e.g. "charlie") who was never added to the group and pass it
  to `joinGroup` with the real `Welcome` -- `decryptGroupSecrets` fails to
  find a matching recipient and throws before any other step runs.
