## 2026-07-23 - US-012

- Zeroized consumed secret tree material and derived message keys so
  forward secrecy no longer depends on GC timing:
  - `src/secret-tree.ts`: `createRatchetResultWithSecret` (the single
    shared tail of both `createRatchetResult`'s sequential-consume path and
    `ratchetToGeneration`'s out-of-order-consume path) now zeroizes the
    just-consumed `secret` parameter after it has been used to derive the
    key/nonce/next-secret. This covers both AC bullets ("a ratchet
    advances" and "out-of-order generation secrets removed from
    unusedGenerations") with one change, since both call sites funnel
    through this function.
  - `src/secret-tree.ts`: `removeOldGenerations` now zeroizes every evicted
    (not-retained) generation's secret buffer before dropping it from the
    returned record.
  - `src/message-protection.ts`: `protect` and `unprotectPrivateMessage`
    each zeroize their local `key`/`nonce` buffers immediately after the
    `cs.hpke.encryptAead`/`decryptAead` call. `encryptSenderData`/
    `decryptSenderData` (private-message.ts) derive a *separate*
    sender-data key/nonce pair via their own `cs.hpke.encryptAead`/
    `decryptAead` call -- out of scope per the AC, which names only
    `src/message-protection.ts`.
- Files changed: `src/secret-tree.ts`, `src/message-protection.ts`,
  `test/validation/key-material-retention.ts`.
- Added 5 new test groups (one per ciphersuite) to
  `test/validation/key-material-retention.ts`:
  - `consumeRatchet` zeroizes the superseded secret in place while leaving
    the returned new tree's ratcheted-forward secret intact.
  - `ratchetToGeneration` zeroizes a consumed out-of-order generation
    secret and removes it from `unusedGenerations`.
  - An evicted (retention-limit-exceeded) generation secret is zeroized
    before being dropped.
  - `protectApplicationData` zeroizes its derived content key/nonce after
    the AEAD call (verified via a wrapped `cs.hpke.encryptAead` that
    captures the arguments by reference).
  - `unprotectPrivateMessage` zeroizes its derived content key/nonce after
    the AEAD call (same technique, wrapping `decryptAead`).
- Updated the pre-existing test
  `addHistoricalReceiverData does not corrupt the previous ClientState
  secretTree` in the same file: it previously asserted the committer's own
  handshake ratchet secret was byte-for-byte unchanged after `createCommit`
  encrypts and sends the commit. That assumption no longer holds now that
  the committer's own just-consumed generation is deliberately zeroized
  (this fix), even though the pre-commit `ClientState` object remains
  reachable via the test's retained `aliceGroup` reference. Restructured
  the test to assert the *new*, correct invariant: the consumed secret is
  now all-zero (not merely "unchanged"), and a second, separate assertion
  -- snapshotting *after* the commit and calling `addHistoricalReceiverData`
  directly -- proves that function itself introduces no *further*
  mutation, which was the test's original real intent.
- Verified targeted test (703 assertions across all ciphersuites for
  `test/validation/key-material-retention.ts`, plus reran
  `test/scenario/generation-out-of-order.ts` (95),
  `test/test-vectors/secret-tree.ts` (3486),
  `test/scenario/join-group-init-key-zeroization.ts` (76), and
  `test/scenario/large-group-full-lifecycle.ts` (4256) as regression
  checks), `npm run lint` (clean), `npx tsc --noEmit` (only the
  pre-existing unrelated `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)` error),
  and full `npm test` (44863 assertions, all passing).

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- **Critical gotcha discovered by this task**: `ClientState` is
  functional/immutable by design (see the Codebase Patterns note on
  `updateArray` and shared node objects), and this codebase has existing
  regression tests that hold onto a pre-operation `ClientState` and assert
  its internals are byte-for-byte unchanged after calling an operation like
  `createCommit`. Any new in-place zeroization of a `Uint8Array` that is
  aliased into the *input* tree (not just the *returned* new tree) will
  silently corrupt that "old" state for as long as any reference to it
  survives -- including in a test, and including in real application code
  that hasn't reassigned `state = result.newState` yet. Before adding
  `.fill(0)` anywhere in a ratchet/tree function, check whether the buffer
  being zeroed is the *exact same object* referenced by the pre-call
  `ClientState`/tree passed in (not just structurally similar) -- spread
  (`{...node, x: y}`) and object-rest destructuring
  (`const {[k]: _, ...rest} = obj`) copy the *container* but keep the same
  *value* references, so a field left untouched by the spread is still
  shared with the old object.
- When a zeroization fix legitimately changes what a pre-existing
  regression test should assert (as opposed to accidentally breaking it),
  update the test to the new, correct invariant rather than reverting the
  fix -- see US-005's precedent in this same file. Isolate the *specific*
  new behavior (e.g. "commit encryption zeroizes the committer's own
  consumed secret") from the *original* intent of the test (e.g.
  "addHistoricalReceiverData itself doesn't mutate") by re-snapshotting
  immediately after the newly-intentional mutation and asserting no
  *further* change happens beyond that point.
- To test that a private function's local `key`/`nonce` buffers get
  zeroized after use (they aren't part of any return value, so there's no
  direct way to inspect them), wrap the relevant `cs.hpke.encryptAead`/
  `decryptAead` method to capture its arguments *by reference* before
  delegating to the real implementation, then assert on the captured array
  after the outer call returns -- the same object is being mutated in
  place, so the capture stays live. Watch out for functions that call the
  wrapped method more than once for different purposes (e.g. `protect`
  calls `encryptAead` once for content and again for sender data, in that
  order; `unprotectPrivateMessage`/`decryptSenderData` call `decryptAead`
  for sender data *first* and content *second* -- the opposite order) --
  record every call and pick the right one by position rather than
  overwriting a single captured variable.
- `ClientState.senderDataSecret` is not a top-level field --  it's
  `state.keySchedule.senderDataSecret`.
- `structuredClone` works fine on a `SecretTree` (array of
  `{handshake, application}` objects holding `Uint8Array`s) and is the
  simplest way to snapshot an independent, unconsumed copy of a tree for a
  test that needs to simulate a second, separate recipient decrypting the
  same message without being affected by the sender's own zeroization.
