## 2026-07-23 - US-013

- `PrivateKeyPath.privateKeys` (`src/private-key-path.ts`) is a
  `Record<nodeIndex, Uint8Array>` of HPKE node private keys, built up over a
  member's lifetime in the group via `mergePrivateKeyPaths`. Two related gaps:
  (1) `mergePrivateKeyPaths` used a plain object spread (`{...a, ...b}`), so
  whenever `b` supplied a fresh key for a node index `a` already held (e.g. a
  self-update refreshing the committer's own direct-path keys), the old,
  now-superseded buffer was simply dropped and left for GC instead of
  zeroized; (2) nothing ever removed an entry once its node was blanked by a
  later commit (e.g. a `remove` proposal blanking the removed leaf's entire
  direct path to root) -- a node that is a copath (not direct-path) node for
  every subsequent committer never gets refreshed, so a stale, permanently
  unusable private key just accumulated in the record forever.
- Fix: `mergePrivateKeyPaths` now iterates `b`'s entries and zeroizes (`.fill(0)`)
  any same-node-index buffer in `a` it overwrites (skipping the case where
  both sides already reference the identical buffer object, to avoid
  double-processing). Added a new export `pruneBlankedNodes(path, tree)` that
  drops and zeroizes any entry whose node index is `undefined` (blank) in the
  given post-mutation `RatchetTree` -- called at every site that finalizes a
  `PrivateKeyPath` after applying a commit: the committer's own path in
  `create-commit.ts` (against the final post-update-path `tree`), and the
  receiver's `applyTreeUpdate`/`updatePrivateKeyPath` in `process-messages.ts`
  (against `updatedTree` for both the member-sender and new-member-commit
  branches, and against the proposal-mutated `tree` for the no-update-path
  branch).
- Deliberately did NOT add pruning to `client-state.ts`'s
  `deriveUpdatedPrivateKeyPath` (used only by `joinGroup`'s Welcome path) or
  `joinGroupExternal`'s freshly-built `privateKeyPath` -- both construct a
  brand-new `PrivateKeyPath` from scratch at join time, so there is no prior
  state to accumulate stale entries from.
- Confirmed it is safe to zeroize buffers reachable from a pre-commit
  `state.privatePath` in place (unlike `secretTree`/`historicalReceiverData`,
  which the Codebase Patterns note above warns against mutating for a still-
  live old `ClientState`): grepped every read of `.privatePath.privateKeys`
  in `src/` and confirmed the only readers are the *current* state's own
  commit-processing code (`applyUpdatePathSecret`'s HPKE decrypt lookup) --
  nothing in this codebase ever reads a *historical* `ClientState`'s
  `privatePath` after a newer state has been built, so there is no equivalent
  of `historicalReceiverData`'s out-of-order-decryption use case to protect.
- Files changed: `src/private-key-path.ts`, `src/create-commit.ts`,
  `src/process-messages.ts`, `test/validation/key-material-retention.ts`.
- Added `blankedNodePrivateKeyIsDroppedAndZeroized` (one run per ciphersuite)
  to `test/validation/key-material-retention.ts`: builds a 4-member group
  (alice creates; bob, charlie, dave added in one commit), has charlie
  self-update (populating her own privatePath with a fresh key for the node
  she shares with dave but not alice -- a copath node relative to every
  future commit alice makes), then has alice remove dave. After charlie
  processes alice's remove commit, for every node index charlie held a key
  for before the commit that is now blank in her post-commit `ratchetTree`,
  asserts the entry is gone from her new `privatePath.privateKeys` and the
  original (captured-by-reference) buffer is now all-zero. A sanity
  assertion confirms at least one such blanked node was actually found (node
  index 5 in every ciphersuite's tree, given the fixed 4-member shape), so
  the test cannot vacuously pass by never checking anything.
- Verified non-tautological: `git stash push -- src/private-key-path.ts
  src/create-commit.ts src/process-messages.ts`, reran the targeted test --
  the two new per-ciphersuite assertions failed (key not dropped, buffer not
  zeroized) on every ciphersuite; `git stash pop` restored the fix and all
  passed.
- Ran the targeted test directly (esbuild+node per the codebase pattern):
  779/779 assertions pass. Ran `npm run lint` (clean) and `npx tsc --noEmit
  -p tsconfig.json` (only the pre-existing unrelated
  `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)` error). Reran
  `test/scenario/remove.ts` (209), `test/scenario/require-update-path.ts`
  (114), `test/scenario/large-group-full-lifecycle.ts` (4256), and
  `test/scenario/leave-proposal.ts` (456) as regression checks -- all pass,
  confirming keys still needed by the new epoch are unaffected. Ran the full
  suite (`npm test`): 44939 assertions, all passing (up from 44863 in the
  US-012 run, i.e. +76, matching the new test's 4-assertions-times-19-
  ciphersuites count).

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- Before assuming a "never mutate a buffer that might be aliased into an old
  `ClientState`" caution (per the US-005/US-012 Codebase Patterns note)
  applies to a given field, actually grep every read site of that field
  across `src/`: `privatePath.privateKeys` (unlike `secretTree` or
  `historicalReceiverData`) has no historical/out-of-order read path, so
  zeroizing superseded/blanked entries in place -- even ones that happen to
  be shared with a still-referenced older `ClientState` -- is safe and is
  exactly the fix this story asked for.
- `removeLeafNode` (`src/ratchet-tree.ts`) blanks the *entire direct path*
  from the removed leaf to the root, not just the leaf itself -- but the
  committer's own `createUpdatePath` immediately refreshes every node on
  the committer's own direct path (which always includes the root), so only
  the nodes strictly between the removed leaf and its first common ancestor
  with the committer end up *permanently* blank after the commit completes.
  To construct a test where a specific member's `privatePath` entry survives
  a remove as newly-stale, pick a victim who is a "cousin" of the removed
  leaf (shares a low ancestor with it, not just the root) and give that
  member a private key for that shared ancestor via their own prior
  self-update (`createUpdatePath` populates every node on the caller's own
  direct path, not just nodes overlapping with the current committer).
