**Learnings for future iterations:**
- `nodeToLeafIndex`/`leafToNodeIndex` round-trip *exactly* through floating
  point for any input (`(n/2)*2 === n` even when `n` is odd and `n/2` is a
  `.5` value) -- this let a genuine invariant violation (a parent node index
  being treated as a leaf index) hide silently for as long as the only
  operations performed on the intermediate fractional value were "convert
  straight back." Before assuming a validation gap is benign because "the
  math happens to work out," check whether that's a real invariant or an
  accidental cancellation that would break the moment someone does
  anything else with the intermediate value (e.g. uses it as a Record key,
  compares it, or serializes it).
- When a story asks you to make an existing helper throw on previously-
  tolerated input, grep every call site of that helper before changing it
  (`grep -rn nodeToLeafIndex src/`) -- one caller (`findFirstNonBlankAncestor`)
  was relying on the old permissive behavior for a legitimate reason (any
  NodeIndex, not just leaves, needed handling), and a second, unrelated
  *test* (`leaf-width-update-path.ts`) was deliberately invoking the old
  buggy behavior as a regression demonstration and needed its assertion
  updated to match the new, stricter contract.
- To verify a "throw a clearer error" story is a genuine improvement (not
  a no-op) when the old code path already happened to throw some error of
  the same error class before your fix, don't rely on `instanceof
  ValidationError` alone in the verification step -- write a standalone
  script that calls the function directly and diff `error.message` (or
  `error.constructor.name` plus message) before and after your change.
