- Added `test/scenario/external-commit-proposal-restrictions.ts` (one run
  per ciphersuite, both tests): calls the exported `applyProposals`
  directly with `senderType: 'new_member_commit'` rather than forging a
  full signed wire message, since `applyProposals` is itself the unit under
  test and is already an exported production function -- no need to route
  through a real `createCommit`/`processPublicMessage` round trip.
  - "rejects proposal delivered by reference": seeds
    `state.unappliedProposals` (via the exported `addUnappliedProposal`
    from `src/unapplied-proposals.ts`) with an `external_init` proposal,
    then calls `applyProposals` with a `proposals` array containing only a
    `{ proposalOrRefType: 'reference', reference }` pointing at it. Uses
    `external_init` (not some other proposal type) specifically so the
    referenced proposal alone is enough to make `isExternalInit` true and
    enter the branch under test, without needing any other by-value
    proposal alongside it.
  - "rejects duplicate PSK proposal IDs": two by-value `psk` proposals
    (`psktype: 'external'`) sharing the same `pskId`, plus an external_init
    proposal, all passed by value (`proposalOrRefType: 'proposal'`) so the
    by-value check doesn't preempt this test.
- Verified non-tautological the hard way: the first version of both tests
  passed even before touching `src/client-state.ts`, but for the wrong
  reason each time -- debugged by temporarily logging the caught error.
  Iteration 1 used an `add` proposal for the by-reference test, which
  `validateExternalInit` already rejects with `'Invalid proposals'`
  (external commits only ever allow `remove`/`psk`/`external_init`
  proposal types) regardless of by-reference/by-value. Iteration 2 used a
  `psk` proposal for the by-reference test and a bare 16-byte `pskNonce`,
  which failed for an unrelated reason (`pskNonce` length must equal
  `cs.kdf.size`, not a fixed 16 bytes). Iteration 3 fixed the nonce length
  but both tests used `emptyPskIndex`, so they failed with `'Could not find
  pskId referenced in proposal'` from `accumulatePskSecret` before ever
  reaching the code under test. Final version: switched the by-reference
  test to reference an `external_init` proposal (sidesteps PSK machinery
  entirely), and gave the duplicate-PSK test a real `makePskIndex(undefined,
  { [base64(pskId)]: <kdf.size-byte secret> })` so `accumulatePskSecret`
  would have succeeded under the old code -- confirmed by stashing
  `src/client-state.ts` and rerunning: all 38 assertions failed (both
  throwing unrelated `CryptoError`s from `importSecret` on the garbage
  `external_init` kemOutput, which is only reached *after* the point where
  the fix's checks now intercept), then passed after unstashing.
- Ran the targeted test directly (esbuild+node per the codebase pattern):
  38/38 assertions pass across all 19 ciphersuites. Reran
  `external-join.ts` (285), `external-join-resync.ts` (323),
  `external-commit-unrelated-remove.ts` (95), `external-add-proposal.ts`
  (209), `external-psk.ts` (76), `external-psk-join.ts` (76),
  `external-proposal.ts` (38), `external-proposal-restricted-types.ts`
  (76), `external-init-sender-policy.ts` (76), `external-sender-policy.ts`
  (76), `custom-proposal.ts` (95), `custom-proposal-rejection.ts` (57),
  `required-capabilites.ts` (57), and `reinit.ts` (133) as regression
  checks for the `validateNoDuplicatePskIds` extraction -- all pass. `npm
  run lint`: clean. `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`: only the
  pre-existing unrelated `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)` error
  (plus one new error caught and fixed during this task -- see Learnings).
  Full suite (`npm test`): 44996 assertions, all passing (up from 44958 in
  the US-014 run, i.e. +38, matching the new test's 2-assertions-times-19-
  ciphersuites count).

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- The crypto `Hash` interface (`src/crypto/hash.ts`) has no `size`/`Nh`
  field -- only `digest`/`mac`/`verifyMac` methods. For an arbitrary
  fixed-length byte buffer in a test that doesn't need to match a real
  digest length (e.g. a fake proposal reference used purely as an
  `unappliedProposals` lookup key), just pick a plausible constant length
  (32) rather than reaching for a ciphersuite property that doesn't exist.
  `CiphersuiteImpl.kdf.size` *does* exist and *is* the correct length for
  anything that must round-trip through real validation, such as a
  `PreSharedKeyID.pskNonce` (checked against `cs.kdf.size` deep in PSK
  secret accumulation) or a PSK secret value returned from
  `makePskIndex`'s `externalPsks` map.
- `applyProposals` (`src/client-state.ts`) is exported and takes every
  dependency (`state`, raw `proposals:ProposalOrRef[]`, `pskSearch`,
  `senderType`, etc.) as a plain argument -- for a unit test that targets
  logic inside this function specifically (as opposed to an end-to-end
  wire-message scenario), call it directly instead of forging a signed
  `PublicMessage`/`PrivateMessage`. This is far simpler than the
  forge-and-resign technique used in US-004/US-007 and is appropriate
  whenever the acceptance criteria name the function itself, not a message
  type.
- External commits (`isExternalInit` branch) only ever accept
  `remove`/`psk`/`external_init` proposal types -- `validateExternalInit`
  rejects `add`/`update`/`group_context_extensions`/`reinit` outright
  (`'Invalid proposals'`). When constructing a test proposal for this
  branch, pick from that allowed set or the test will fail for an unrelated
  reason before ever reaching the logic under test.
- When a test that's supposed to fail before a fix instead passes
  immediately, don't trust the green checkmark -- temporarily log the
  caught error's message/stack (or `error.constructor.name`) to confirm
  *which* validation actually fired. This caught two dead-end iterations in
  this task (`'Invalid proposals'` from the wrong proposal type, then
  `'Could not find pskId referenced in proposal'` from an unpopulated
  `pskSearch`) before the test was actually exercising the intended code
  path.
