## 2026-07-23 - US-001

- Excluded `/docs` from client-side routing so links to the typedoc output
  load as real pages instead of being caught by `route-event`.
- Extracted the routing decision into a new pure function `isDocsPath(href,
  basePath)` in `example/routing.ts`: strips any query string off `href`,
  normalizes `basePath` (strips a trailing slash), and checks whether the
  remaining pathname equals `<base>/docs` or starts with `<base>/docs/`.
  Kept this logic out of `example/state.ts` deliberately -- that module
  calls `Route()` at import time (a DOM-dependent side effect), so anything
  defined there can't be unit-tested by importing it as a value in the
  Node test bundle (only type-only imports from `state.ts` are currently
  safe, per the existing `test/example/participants.ts` pattern).
- `example/state.ts`: `Route()` now takes a `handleLink` option:
  `handleLink: (href) => !isDocsPath(href, basePath)`, where `basePath =
  import.meta.env.BASE_URL` (so dev's `/` and the Pages build's
  `/webcrypto-mls/` both work without hard-coding either). Confirmed via
  `node_modules/route-event/dist/catch-links.js` that `href` passed to
  `handleLink` is `url.pathname + url.search` (already same-origin
  checked) and that returning `false` skips `preventDefault`, letting the
  browser handle the click natively -- exactly the "return false" contract
  the acceptance criteria describe.
- Files changed: `example/routing.ts` (new), `example/state.ts`,
  `test/example/routing.ts` (new), `test/index.ts` (wired new test file).
- Added `test/example/routing.ts` (12 assertions): exact/nested/trailing-
  slash matches for both the dev (`/`) and Pages (`/webcrypto-mls/`) base
  paths, a non-docs path under each base, and two query-string cases
  (a docs path with a query string still matches; a non-docs path whose
  query string happens to contain the word "docs" does not false-match).
- Verified non-tautological: ran the test file directly (esbuild+node
  pattern) before `example/routing.ts` existed -- esbuild failed to
  resolve the import, confirming the test exercises real, not-yet-written
  code; then implemented and reran, 12/12 pass.
- `npm run lint` clean. `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json` shows only the
  pre-existing unrelated `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)` error
  (per the Codebase Patterns note above). Full suite (`npm test`):
  45026/45026 assertions pass.
- Browser verification: ran `npm run build-pages` (emits `public/` with
  base `/webcrypto-mls/` and `public/docs/` from typedoc), served it at
  that path prefix (a symlink `webcrypto-mls -> public` under a temp
  static-server root, since the built HTML references
  `/webcrypto-mls/assets/...`), and used Playwright to inject a
  `/webcrypto-mls/docs/` link and a `/webcrypto-mls/persistence` link
  (index.html doesn't have real nav links yet -- US-003 adds a shared
  nav). Clicking the docs link changed the page title to the typedoc
  page's title (`@vanishing.page/webcrypto-mls`), confirming a real
  document load; clicking the other link changed the URL via history but
  left a `window.__testMarker` value set before the click intact,
  confirming client-side routing (no reload) for non-docs links. Cleaned
  up the temp static server and symlink afterward.

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- `route-event`'s `handleLink` option (`Route({ handleLink: (href) =>
  boolean })`) receives `url.pathname + url.search` for same-origin link
  clicks only (`catch-links.js` already filters out different-host
  links before calling it) and returning `false` causes the click handler
  to return early without `preventDefault`, i.e. the browser processes
  the click as a normal navigation. Returning `true` (or omitting the
  option) lets `route-event` intercept it for client-side routing.
- Any logic added to `example/state.ts` cannot be unit-tested by
  importing the module at runtime in the Node test bundle, because the
  module executes `Route()` (a DOM-dependent side effect) at import time.
  `test/example/participants.ts` already works around this by only
  importing `type User` from `state.ts` (erased at compile, no runtime
  import). For anything that needs a real runtime import in a test
  (like this task's routing predicate), extract it into its own
  side-effect-free module (see `example/routing.ts`) and import *that*
  from both `state.ts` and the test.
- To browser-verify a built GitHub Pages-style output locally: `npm run
  build-pages`, then serve `public/` from a directory structure that
  mirrors the Pages URL layout, since the built `index.html`'s asset
  `src`/`href` paths are already prefixed with the `--base` used at build
  time (`/webcrypto-mls/` here) -- e.g. symlink
  `<tmp-root>/webcrypto-mls -> public` and serve `<tmp-root>` with a
  static file server, then browse to `http://localhost:PORT/webcrypto-mls/`.
