  -- fixed via `eslint --fix example/nav.ts`; clean afterward. `npx tsc
  --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`: only the pre-existing unrelated
  `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)` error (per Codebase Patterns).
- Browser verification (Playwright):
  - Dev server (`npx vite`): loaded `/`, confirmed the nav renders three
    links with correct hrefs (`/`, `/persistence`, `/docs/`) and "Main demo"
    marked active. Clicked "Persistence": URL changed to `/persistence` via
    History API, a `window` marker set before the click survived (no full
    reload), the nav's active link flipped to "Persistence", and the
    placeholder "Persistence Demo" heading rendered. Clicked "Main demo":
    returned to `/` client-side.
  - Built-and-served Pages output (`npm run build-pages`, served via the
    same symlink-to-a-`webcrypto-mls`-prefixed-directory technique as the
    US-001/US-002 log entries): confirmed nav hrefs are correctly prefixed
    (`/webcrypto-mls/`, `/webcrypto-mls/persistence`, `/webcrypto-mls/docs/`).
    Clicked "Docs": the page title changed to the typedoc page's title
    (`@vanishing.page/webcrypto-mls`) and the pre-click `window` marker was
    gone afterward, confirming a full page load (not intercepted by
    `route-event`), exactly as US-001 established for a plain injected link
    -- this is the first verification that a *real rendered nav link*
    (not an injected test-only `<a>`) triggers the same behavior.
  - Cleaned up the dev server, static file server, and the temporary
    symlink directory afterward.
- Full suite (`npm test`): 45057/45057 assertions pass (up from 45036 in
  the US-002 run, i.e. +21, matching the new test's assertion count).

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- `example/routing.ts`'s split (pure predicate function + DOM-dependent
  component/state module) generalizes cleanly to any new example-app UI that
  needs both unit tests and DOM rendering: put the pure, side-effect-free
  logic in its own module so it can be imported at runtime in the Node test
  bundle, and keep only the actual JSX/`html` template in a component that
  consumes it. `example/nav.ts` (`navItems` + `Nav`) follows this same shape
  in one file rather than splitting into two, since `Nav` itself has no
  DOM-dependent *module-level* side effect (unlike `state.ts`'s `Route()`
  call) -- only `navItems` needed to be independently testable, and it's a
  named export other code (or a future test) could still import without
  pulling in `preact`/`htm` at all.
- The shared nav needed no new click-interception logic: `route-event`'s
  `handleLink` (wired once, at `state.ts` module load, per US-001) already
  governs every same-origin link click document-wide, including ones on
  elements that don't exist yet at that time (event delegation) -- so any
  future nav link just needs the right `href`; whether it's a real vs.
  client-side navigation is entirely decided by `isDocsPath`/
  `isPersistencePath` against that `href`, not by anything in the nav
  component.
- To browser-verify a full page load specifically (as opposed to just "the
  URL changed"), the marker-survives-or-not check from US-001/US-002 is
  reusable verbatim: set `window.__someMarker` before the click, and check
  it's `undefined` afterward. `undefined` (not still `true`) confirms an
  actual document reload happened, not just a same-page DOM mutation that
  happened to also change the URL.
