**Learnings for future iterations:**
- `generateKeyPackage` (`src/key-package.ts`) already accepts an optional
  trailing `options?:{ signatureKeyPair?:CryptoKeyPair }` -- when it's
  supplied, the returned `PrivateKeyPackage.signaturePrivateKey` is
  literally `options.signatureKeyPair.privateKey` (same object reference,
  not re-derived), so a test can assert non-extractability and identity
  directly against the `CryptoKeyPair` it generated, without needing to
  inspect any raw bytes.
- When splitting page-specific state (`example/state.ts`'s `route` signal,
  owned only by the top-level app shell) from cross-page state (group
  messaging), the natural boundary is exactly what US-001's
  `routing.ts`/`nav.ts` split already established: a page-agnostic module
  (`demo-state.ts`/`demo-actions.ts`) holding pure data + async actions
  that any page's component can call, versus a page's own module
  (`state.ts`, `persistence-demo.ts`) that owns only what's specific to
  that page (routing side effects, or which actions aren't offered on this
  page at all).
- `batch()` only helps when multiple *different* signals (or the same
  signal multiple times) are set in the same synchronous tick; a sequence
  of single-signal writes separated by `await` gets no benefit from
  wrapping in `batch` since each `await` yields back to the event loop
  before the next write runs -- don't reflexively wrap every multi-write
  function in `batch` if its writes are already async-interleaved single
  assignments. `state.ts`'s pre-existing `removeUserFromGroup`/
  `rotateKeys` already established this as the codebase's convention for
  such loops.
