## 2026-07-23 - US-005

- This story's implementation was already sitting uncommitted in the
  working tree at session start (`example/persistence-storage.ts` (new),
  `example/persistence-demo.ts`, `test/example/persistence-storage.ts`
  (new), `test/index.ts`). Per the US-003/US-004 precedent, re-verified
  every acceptance criterion against the existing diff line by line
  rather than assuming it was wrong or restarting.
- `example/persistence-storage.ts` (new): a dedicated `mls-persistence-demo`
  indexedDB database (`members` object store), keyed by `memberKey(groupIdB64,
  name)` = `${groupIdB64}:${name}`, following the README's `saveState`/
  `loadState` helper pattern (open/upgrade/transaction/put/get, all wrapped
  in Promises). `saveMember`/`loadAllMembers`/`deleteDatabase` do the
  indexedDB I/O; `restoredUsersFromRecords` (pure, no `indexedDB` global
  needed) composes loaded records back into a `users` Map + `groupId` for
  the demo state, unit-tested directly in Node.
- Deliberate deviation from the README's literal example: `saveMember`
  strips `ClientState.clientConfig` before storing (`const { clientConfig:
  _clientConfig, ...persistableState } = state`) and `restoredUsersFromRecords`
  rebuilds a fresh one (`freshClientConfig()`) on restore. Verified this is
  necessary, not just cautious: `ClientConfig.authService` (`AuthenticationService.
  validateCredential`) and `.keyPackageEqualityConfig` (`compareKeyPackages`/
  `compareKeyPackageToLeafNode`) are method-bearing objects, and the
  structured clone algorithm cannot clone functions -- storing `clientConfig`
  as-is would throw `DataCloneError` on every `saveMember` call. Since the
  demo never mutates `clientConfig` away from its defaults, re-deriving it
  on restore is lossless. `ClientState.signaturePrivateKey` (the
  non-extractable `CryptoKey`) has no such problem and is stored as part of
  `persistableState` unchanged, per the README's structured-clone note.
- `example/persistence-demo.ts`: each member row gets a `persist`/`persisted`
  button (disabled once persisted); `persistMember` saves immediately,
  `syncPersistedMembers` (called after every state-advancing action --
  `decryptMessage`, `addUserToGroup`, `sendMessage`) re-saves every
  already-persisted member's *current* state so a persisted record tracks
  the group's current epoch even for bystander members who only advanced by
  processing someone else's commit. `restoreFromStorage` (awaited before the
  component's first render, right after `initCiphersuite` so the cipher
  suite is always re-derived via `getCipherSuite` regardless of whether
  anything is restored) merges restored users into state, sets `groupId`,
  marks all restored names as already-persisted, and flips
  `restoredFromStorage` to show the "Restored from indexedDB" notice.
  `resetDemo` deletes the database and reloads the page.
- Files changed: `example/persistence-storage.ts` (new),
  `example/persistence-demo.ts`, `test/example/persistence-storage.ts` (new),
  `test/index.ts` (wired new test file), `specs/prd.json`.
- `test/example/persistence-storage.ts` (9 assertions, already present,
  verified rather than rewritten): `memberKey` joins groupId/name with a
  colon; `restoredUsersFromRecords` builds a correct users Map + derives
  `groupId` from the last record for both a populated and an empty input.
  Ran directly (esbuild+node pattern): 9/9 pass.
- Grepped both new/changed files for `console.` -- none found, satisfying
  the "no key material or other sensitive data is logged" criterion.
- `npm run lint`: clean. `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`: only the
  pre-existing unrelated `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)` error.
  Full suite (`npm test`): 45083/45083 assertions pass.
- Browser verification (Playwright against `npx vite`, port 5183, on
  `/persistence`): created Alice and Bob, started the group as Alice
  (Epoch 0, persist button appears), added Bob (both reach Epoch 1),
  clicked `persist` on both (buttons become disabled "persisted").
  Reloaded the page: "Restored from indexedDB" notice shown, both Alice
  and Bob restored at Epoch 1 with the same group ID, both already marked
  persisted. Typed a message and sent it as the *restored* Alice, then
  decrypted it as the *restored* Bob -- confirms the restored `ClientState`
  is directly usable to advance the group (no re-join needed), matching
  the acceptance criterion's "epochs advance from the restored states."
  Clicked Reset: database deleted, page reloaded to the fresh empty state
  (0 users, Create buttons back, no restored notice). Cleaned up the dev
  server afterward.

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- `ClientConfig`'s `authService` and `keyPackageEqualityConfig` fields hold
  object literals with methods (`validateCredential`, `compareKeyPackages`,
  `compareKeyPackageToLeafNode`) -- the structured clone algorithm used by
  `indexedDB.objectStore.put` cannot clone functions, so any code persisting
  a whole `ClientState` via structured clone (as the README's `saveState`
  suggests) must strip `clientConfig` first and re-derive it from the
  application's own defaults on load, rather than storing it verbatim. This
  is safe precisely because `ClientConfig` is meant to be an
  application-wide constant, not per-member state that could have diverged.
- `ClientState.signaturePrivateKey` (a possibly-non-extractable `CryptoKey`)
  and every other field of `ClientState` *except* `clientConfig` structured-
  clone without issue -- confirmed by the full persist/reload/send/decrypt
  round trip above completing with no `DataCloneError` anywhere.
- To browser-verify that a *restored* (not freshly-created) `ClientState` is
  genuinely live and usable, don't stop at "the UI shows the restored data"
  -- drive a real state advance (send + decrypt a message) using only the
  post-reload page state, the same technique this session used, mirroring
  the epoch-advance requirement in this story's acceptance criteria.
