  it's hidden entirely when `storageStatus.value === 'unsupported'` and
  disabled when already `'persistent'`. A denied request just leaves the
  status at `'best-effort'` -- no error path, matching the acceptance
  criterion that denial is a normal outcome.
- Files changed: `example/storage-persistence.ts` (new),
  `example/persistence-demo.ts`, `test/example/storage-persistence.ts`
  (new), `test/index.ts` (wired new test file), `specs/prd.json`.
- Added `test/example/storage-persistence.ts` (11 assertions): mocks
  `globalThis.navigator` via `Object.defineProperty(globalThis, 'navigator',
  { value: ..., configurable: true })` (Node's built-in `navigator` global
  is otherwise non-writable) to cover all four `StorageStatus` outcomes for
  both functions, plus the "no `navigator.storage` at all" and "`storage`
  object present but missing the specific method" unsupported cases
  separately. Confirmed the test fails first (`storage-persistence.js`
  didn't exist yet -- esbuild import-resolution error), then implemented
  until all 11 passed.
- Ran the targeted test directly (esbuild+node pattern): 11/11 pass. `npm
  run lint`: one `@stylistic/space-infix-ops` violation on the
  `StorageManager|null` return type, fixed via `eslint --fix
  example/storage-persistence.ts` (needs a space around the union `|`,
  unlike this codebase's no-space colon-type-annotation convention -- the
  two are separate stylistic rules). `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`:
  only the pre-existing unrelated `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)`
  error. Full suite (`npm test`): 45094/45094 assertions pass.
- Browser verification (Playwright against `npx vite`, port 5199, on
  `/persistence`): fresh load showed "Storage: best-effort" with an enabled
  "Request persistent storage" button. Clicked it: headless Chromium denies
  `navigator.storage.persist()` by default (no site-engagement history), so
  the status correctly stayed "Storage: best-effort" with no thrown error
  and the button still enabled (not yet persistent) -- confirming denial is
  handled as a normal outcome per the acceptance criteria, though this
  environment could not exercise the granted/disabled-button branch
  directly (covered instead by the unit tests' mocked `persist: async () =>
  true` case). Only console noise was an unrelated `favicon.ico` 404.
  Cleaned up the dev server afterward.

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- Node's global `navigator` object exists (Node 21+) but its `storage`
  property is not natively settable -- `Object.defineProperty(globalThis,
  'navigator', { value: {...}, configurable: true })` (redefining the whole
  property, not assigning into it) is the reliable way to stub
  `navigator.storage` for a Node-run unit test.
- ESLint's `@stylistic/space-infix-ops` requires spaces around a TypeScript
  union type operator (`StorageManager | null`), which looks inconsistent
  next to this codebase's no-space colon-type-annotation style
  (`name:type`) from CLAUDE.md -- they're independent stylistic rules covering
  different tokens (`:` vs `|`), not a contradiction; `eslint --fix` handles
  it correctly without needing a manual style judgment call.
- All this session's `example/*.ts` top-level modules already do real async
  work at module load (`await initCiphersuite(state)` in both demo state
  modules, `await restoreFromStorage()` in `persistence-demo.ts` per
  US-005) -- adding another top-level `await checkPersisted()` for the
  initial storage status fits the same established pattern rather than
  introducing a new one (e.g. an effect or a loading-state signal).
