## 2026-07-26 - US-001 (multi-device-demo)

New run: `ralph/multi-device-demo`. Source PRD:
`tasks/prd-multi-device-demo.md`.

- Added `example/devices.ts`, the pure device model every later
  multi-device story builds on: `DeviceId` (`'phone'|'laptop'|'desktop'`),
  a `Device` interface (`id`, `label`, `icon`), the ordered `DEVICES`
  list (phone 📱, laptop 💻, desktop 🖥️), `clientId(user, deviceId)`
  producing `'Alice/phone'`, `parseClientId(id)` returning
  `{ user, deviceId }` or `null`, and `userClientIds(user)` returning a
  person's three ids in `DEVICES` order.
- `parseClientId` splits on `'/'` and requires exactly two parts, so a
  missing separator, an extra separator (`'Alice/phone/extra'`), an empty
  user, an empty device id, and an unrecognized or wrong-case device id
  all return `null` rather than throwing. It returns `null` (not a thrown
  `ValidationError`) because this is demo view-layer code parsing its own
  identifiers, not `src/` parsing untrusted wire input -- the `src/`
  ValidationError convention does not apply here.
- Test-first: wrote `test/example/devices.ts` and confirmed it failed to
  even bundle (`Could not resolve "../../example/devices.js"`) before
  writing the module. Covers the round trip
  `parseClientId(clientId(u, d))` for all seven `EXAMPLE_USERS` x all
  three devices, the eight malformed-id rejections, `DEVICES` ordering,
  and `userClientIds` ordering. 54/54 assertions pass.
- Registered `test/example/devices.js` in `test/index.ts` as a
  side-effect import.
- Files changed: `example/devices.ts` (new),
  `test/example/devices.ts` (new), `test/index.ts`, `specs/prd.json`.
- `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`: only the pre-existing unrelated
  `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)` error. `npm run lint`: clean.
  Full suite `npm test`: 45172/45172 pass.
- No browser verification for this story -- the module is pure and this
  acceptance-criteria list (unlike US-002 onward) does not ask for it.

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- Client ids in the multi-device demo are `'<User>/<deviceId>'`. The
  demo's `users` Map is keyed by client id, not by person, so anything
  that needs the person back out of a map key must go through
  `parseClientId` -- do not re-split on `'/'` by hand at call sites.
- `EXAMPLE_USERS` (`example/example-users.ts`) is plain data with no
  browser dependencies, so node-bundled tests under `test/example/` can
  import it directly to drive table-style coverage over all seven people.
- Cheap way to satisfy the "write a failing test first" rule for a
  brand-new module: run the esbuild targeted-test command before the
  module exists. The unresolved-import bundle failure is the red step.
