  module-level exports, so no call site can reach an unbound version. The
  hardcoded `DB_NAME` constant is gone; `example/persistence-demo.ts` now
  names its own database (`mls-persistence-demo`) where it builds its store.
- The pure exports (`memberKey`, `restoredUsersFromRecords`,
  `partitionPersistedNames`, `partitionRestorableRecords`, the
  `PersistedMember` type) stayed top-level with unchanged signatures, so
  `test/example/persistence-storage.ts` needed no edit.
- New `MemberStore` interface types the four bound operations.
- Files changed: `example/persistence-storage.ts`,
  `example/persistence-demo.ts`, `test/index.ts`; new
  `test/example/member-store.ts`.
- Test-first: the new test file was written against `createMemberStore`
  before it existed, and the targeted esbuild command failed with
  "No matching export ... for import createMemberStore" as the red step.
  14 assertions now cover a save/load/delete round trip, `clientConfig`
  being stripped before storage, two stores with different `dbName` values
  not seeing each other's records, `deleteDatabase` dropping only its own
  database, group-scoped keying, and the factory staying lazy (constructing
  a store in Node, which has no `indexedDB`, must not throw).
- Verified in a real browser: created Alice, started the group, persisted,
  read indexedDB directly (one database, `mls-persistence-demo`, one record
  keyed `<groupIdB64>:Alice`), then reloaded -- "Restored 1 member(s) from
  indexedDB", 1 member in group, persist button disabled and reading
  "persisted". Full suite: 45186 assertions pass. Lint clean. Typecheck
  reports only the pre-existing `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)`
  error.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - A demo page can build its store at module scope only because the
    factory defers `indexedDB.open` until an operation is called. Keep it
    that way: `persistence-demo.ts` destructures the store at module top
    level, above the top-level `await restoreFromStorage()`.
  - `restoreFromStorage` in `persistence-demo.ts` is the reference shape
    for US-021: it catches everything into `state.status` because it runs
    under a top-level `await` in a statically-imported module, where an
    escaping rejection would take down the whole app including the Reset
    button.
  - The multi-device page (US-019) should build its store the same way
    with `dbName: 'mls-multi-device-demo'`; the isolation the story asks
    for is already covered by the new test.
  - No `AGENTS.md` exists anywhere in this repo -- the `## Codebase
    Patterns` section at the top of this file plays that role, so reusable
