  and once a group is active the buttons are replaced by a line saying
  so -- there is one group per page, as on the other two demos.
- `startGroupAs(user)` in `example/multi-device-demo.ts` runs the plan
  from the new pure helper: `createMLSGroup(state, plan.creator)` for
  the phone, then one `addUserToGroup(state, plan.creator, joiner)` per
  remaining device. Both come from `demo-actions.ts` unchanged -- a
  device is just a client name to them. The founder commits each add,
  and `addUserToGroup`'s broadcast loop carries every earlier joiner
  forward, so all three land at the same epoch (2 after two adds).
- New pure export in `example/household.ts`:
  `householdStartPlan(rows)` -> `{ creator, joiners }` or null. The
  creator is the first *created* device in DEVICES order (the phone,
  once the whole household exists) and the joiners are the rest in the
  same order. Written test-first: the targeted esbuild bundle failed
  with "No matching export ... householdStartPlan" as the red step.
  Six new assertions cover the full household, the empty one, and a
  partial household where only the laptop exists.
- Status card additions: an "Epoch: N" line derived from *every*
  in-group client, not one of them. `groupEpochs` collects the distinct
  epochs into a set, so the label reads "Epoch" while the devices agree
  and "Epochs: 1, 2" if one ever falls behind. That is what makes the
  "all three at the same epoch" criterion checkable in the browser
  rather than inferred.
- Device rows in User Management now carry an "in group" badge
  (`.list.households .devices .in-group`, using the existing
  `--color-surface-success` / `--color-text-success` pair). The row
  markup moved into a `deviceRow` helper.
- Files changed: `example/multi-device-demo.ts`, `example/household.ts`,
  `example/style.css`, `test/example/household.ts`, `specs/prd.json`.
  No changes to `src/`.
- `npm run lint`: clean. `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`: only the
  pre-existing `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts(73,9)` error.
- Browser verification (Playwright against `npx vite --port 5199`):
  before any devices exist the card says "Create someone's devices
  first". After creating Alice's devices, "Start group as Alice"
  appears; clicking it leaves all three of Alice's rows badged "in
  group", the Status card at "Epoch: 2" (singular -- the three agree),
  and the card switched to the group-active text. Repeated with Alice
  and Bob both created, starting as Bob: Bob's three go in group at a
  single "Epoch: 2" while Alice's three stay out. Zero console errors
  on a clean load. `/persistence` still renders unchanged. Dev server
  killed afterward.

**Learnings for future iterations:**
- `addUserToGroup` already does the whole fan-out: it commits as the
  adder, joins the new member from the welcome, and replays the commit
  into every other in-group client. Calling it once per device is
  therefore enough to keep a household epoch-consistent -- no manual
  broadcast is needed here. US-006's single-commit household add is a
  different code path precisely because it must batch the proposals
  rather than reuse this.
- Read epoch off every in-group client, not the founder. A per-client
  epoch set costs three lines and turns "they should all agree" into
  something the page actually shows; with a single-client read, a
  member silently left behind by `addUserToGroup`'s best-effort
  `catch {}` would look fine.
- Vite HMR keeps stale errors in the Playwright console history: after
  editing mid-session, `browser_console_messages` with `all: true`
  reports ReferenceErrors from intermediate module versions. Reload and
  read only the errors since the last navigation before concluding
  anything about the page.
