## 2026-07-27 - US-006

- Add and remove a whole household with a *single* commit. One person is
  three MLS clients, so "Alice adds Bob's devices" has to resolve into
  one committing client plus three add proposals carried by one commit;
  the epoch then advances once per household rather than once per
  device.
- `example/household.ts` grew the pure planning layer:
  `householdCommitter` (the person's first in-group device in DEVICES
  order -- a person cannot commit, only a client can), `householdAddPlan`
  and `householdRemovePlan` (both returning `{ committer, targets }` or
  null), and `membershipSummary` / `membershipText` for the status line.
  `householdRemovePlan` returns null when the committer is among the
  targets: RFC 9420 forbids a commit that removes its own sender, so a
  household cannot remove itself.
- `example/demo-actions.ts` gained `addUsersToGroup` and
  `removeUsersFromGroup`, the multi-proposal versions of the existing
  single-member actions, plus a shared `deliverCommit` helper that fans
  one commit out to every other member. Both return `boolean` so a
  caller can tell a real commit from a failed one. The existing
  single-member actions are untouched.
- `example/multi-device-demo.ts` renders an "Acting as" select (only
  people holding at least one leaf) and, under it, one add button and
  one remove button per eligible household. The Status card now reads
  `Members in group: 21 (7 people x 3 devices)`.
- Files changed: `example/household.ts`, `example/demo-actions.ts`,
  `example/multi-device-demo.ts`, `test/example/household.ts`,
  `specs/prd.json`.
- Verified in the browser: seven households created (21 devices), group
  started as Alice at epoch 2, then six household adds took it to 21
  members at epoch 8 -- exactly one epoch per household, every client at
  the same epoch. A household removal drops three leaves in one commit,
  and a non-founder (Bob) can commit one too.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - A single `Welcome` covers every joiner of a multi-add commit: each
    decrypts its own `GroupSecrets` by its own key package reference, so
    calling `joinGroup` once per new member against the same Welcome is
    correct and needs no per-member commit.
  - Re-adding a household that was previously removed fails with
    `OpenError`. A removed client keeps its key package in the demo's
    users map, but that key package's init key was already consumed by
    the first Welcome, so the HPKE open fails. This is pre-existing demo
    behavior (`removeUserFromGroup` leaves the same shape), not
    something US-006 introduced -- the device has to be re-created
    first. The failure is now *reported* rather than silent.
  - Wrapping a demo action to write a friendlier status line silently
    turns every failure into a false success, because the action itself
    reports errors through the same `status` signal. Any new wrapper
    must check the action's return value before overwriting the status.
    This is exactly how the OpenError above stayed invisible until the
    membership count was checked against the message.
  - Driving these pages from Playwright: click one button, then poll
    until its label ('Creating...', 'Committing...', 'Starting...')
    disappears. Firing several key-package or commit actions at once
    races on `users.value`, since each is a read-modify-write of the
    same signal.
  - EXAMPLE_USERS are Alice, Bob, Carl, Desmond, Eloise, Fran, Glorg --
    not the Charlie/Diana/Eve set.
