  `example/multi-device-demo.ts`, `example/style.css`,
  `test/example/household.ts`, `progress.log`, `specs/prd.json`.
- Verified in the browser at a full 21-leaf tree: 21 leaves, all 21
  carrying `hide-label`, 0 labels visible at rest. Hovering one leaf
  shows exactly one label ("Desmond's Phone 📱") with a white stroke
  halo; focusing a leaf shows its label and blurring hides it again.
  The main demo's SVG is still `60x60`, viewBox `-30 -30 60 60`, its
  one leaf label visible and reading "Alice". Full suite: 45255 pass.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - SVG has no z-index, so an overlapping revealed label cannot be
    raised above later-painted siblings. It does not need to be: leaf
    labels sit at `y + 28`, below the bottom row of circles, so they
    only ever cross other leaf labels -- and only one is visible. The
    `paint-order: stroke` halo covers the edges they do cross.
  - An SVG root clips to its viewport, so any text wider than the leaf
    slot needs the viewBox margin widened rather than
    `overflow: visible`, which the `.tree-scroll` container would clip
    anyway.
  - Driving the 21-leaf tree: the household add buttons re-render
    between commits, so a list of names captured up front goes stale
    and silently skips people. Loop instead -- click whatever
    `adds ...'s devices` button is present, wait, re-query -- and check
    `.member-count` reads 21 at the end.

