## 2026-07-26 - US-010

- Selecting a person in the multi-device demo now highlights every leaf
  they hold plus each of those leaves' direct paths, using the same
  `on-path` styling a single hovered member gets on the other two pages.
- New pure module `example/household-highlight.ts` exporting
  `householdPathNodeIndices(tree, rows, users)`: it walks the person's
  `DeviceRow[]`, reads `state.privatePath.leafIndex` off each in-group
  client, and unions `directPathNodeIndices` across them. Shared
  ancestors (always at least the root) collapse to one entry because the
  result is a `Set`. No in-group device, or an empty tree, gives an
  empty set.
- `example/multi-device-demo.ts` computes that set in a `useComputed`
  keyed on `selectedPerson` and passes it as `TreeDiagram`'s
  `hoveredPath`, which was previously a hardcoded empty set on this
  page. Nothing in `example/index.ts`, `example/persistence-demo.ts`, or
  `example/tree-diagram.ts` changed, so the single-path hover
  highlighting on the other two pages is untouched.
- Files changed: `example/household-highlight.ts` (new),
  `test/example/household-highlight.ts` (new), `test/index.ts`,
  `example/multi-device-demo.ts`.
- Browser verification: with Alice's three devices founding the group,
  selecting Alice put 11 elements in `.on-path` -- the six nodes of the
  union {0,1,2,3,4,5} plus the five edges whose two endpoints are both
  on it. Selecting Bob (no devices) dropped it to 0 with no console
  error. On the main demo, hovering Alice's Rotate Keys button in a
  one-member group still highlighted exactly her leaf.
- Full suite: 45261 assertions pass. Lint clean. `tsc --noEmit` reports
  only the known pre-existing `test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts` error.

**Learnings for future iterations:**
  - `TreeDiagram`'s `hoveredPath` prop is really "the set of node indices
    to draw as on-path" -- it is not tied to hovering. Any page-specific
    highlight (selection, search, whatever) should feed that prop rather
    than add a second styling mechanism. An edge is highlighted only when
    *both* of its endpoints are in the set, so a union of paths lights up
    the shared trunk automatically.
  - A person's leaf index is not on `DeviceRow`; it lives on
    `users.get(row.clientId).state.privatePath.leafIndex`. So any helper
    mapping a household onto the tree needs the users map as well as the
    rows -- `DeviceRow.inGroup` tells you a leaf exists but not which one.
  - Counting `.on-path` elements in the browser is a good way to check
    tree highlighting without asserting on markup: for a set of N nodes
    you also get one edge per parent/child pair fully inside the set.

## 2026-07-27 - US-011

- Extracted TreeDiagram's card header (title plus Clear button) into a
  shared `CardHeader` component so a future Device Info panel can render
  the same header under a different title. Pure refactor: the markup,
  classes (`.tree-header`, `.clear`), accessible name
  ("Clear selection"), and Clear behavior are unchanged.
- Files changed: `example/card-header.ts` (new),
  `example/tree-diagram.ts`, `test/example/card-header.ts` (new),
  `test/index.ts`.
- Verified in the browser (vite dev server, main demo): header reads
  "Ratchet Tree", no Clear button until a node is selected, Clear
  appears on selecting Alice's leaf and deselects it when clicked.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - Preact components in `example/` can be unit tested in the node
    suite without a DOM or any new dependency: call the component
    function directly (`CardHeader(props, {})`) and walk the returned
    vnode tree (`node.type`, `node.props`, `node.props.children`).
    htm nests children in arrays, so flatten them while walking. Assert
    on structure and props (class, aria-label, onClick), not on
    rendered text, to stay non-brittle.
  - Do NOT type such a vnode walker with a recursive
    `type Child = VNode | ... | Child[]` and call `.flat(Infinity)` on
    it -- tsc reports TS2589 ("type instantiation is excessively
    deep"). Type the walker with `unknown` and flatten by hand.
