## 2026-07-26 - US-021

- The multi-device page now restores its persisted devices on load.
  - New `example/device-restore.ts` holds `partitionDeviceRecords`: it
    splits the store's records by whether `parseClientId` accepts the
    record name, then hands only the accepted ones to
    `partitionRestorableRecords`, concatenating both sets of rejects
    into one `stale` list. Filtering happens *before* the delegation on
    purpose -- `partitionRestorableRecords` trusts its highest-epoch
    record as the authoritative tree, so a foreign record with an
    inflated epoch would otherwise make every real device read as
    stale.
  - `multi-device-demo.ts` gained `restoreDevices()`, called under a
    top-level `await`: it loads the store, deletes every stale record
    at the key its own `groupContext.groupId` reproduces, restores the
    rest with `restoredUsersFromRecords`, and seeds `persistedIds` from
    the restored names. Failures are caught and written to
    `state.status` rather than escaping module evaluation.
  - Nothing about the person-to-device grouping is stored. Records are
    keyed by client id and the users map is keyed by client id, so
    every household view (`selectHousehold` and friends) rebuilds the
    grouping by parsing those ids -- restoring the map restores the
    grouping for free.
- Files changed: `example/device-restore.ts` (new),
  `example/multi-device-demo.ts`, `test/example/device-restore.ts`
  (new), `test/index.ts`, `specs/prd.json`.
- Verified in the browser: created Alice's and Bob's devices, started
  the group, added Bob's household (epoch 3), persisted all three of
  Alice's devices, then seeded a legacy record keyed by the person name
  'Alice'. A hard reload reported 'Restored 3 device(s) from indexedDB
  (dropped 1 stale record(s))' at epoch 3, the Devices section showed
  Alice's three devices in group and persisted, the 'Alice' record was
  gone from the store, and rotating the restored phone committed
  successfully to epoch 4.

**Learnings for future iterations:**
  - A restored client has `state` but no `keyPackage`, so
    `selectHousehold` reports it as `inGroup: true, created: false` and
    the "Create <user>'s devices" button stays on screen for a person
    whose devices were just restored. Clicking it would call
    `createUser`, which *replaces* the map entry and so drops the
    restored `ClientState`. The persistence demo has the identical
    hazard, so this was left alone rather than fixed here -- but
    anything that touches the create path should know about it.
  - Deleting a stale record needs a key, and the only key available is
    `memberKey(bytesToBase64url(record.state.groupContext.groupId),
    record.name)` -- reconstructed from the record itself, as the
    persistence demo does. A record whose `state` is malformed enough
    to lack a `groupContext` therefore cannot be deleted individually;
    it throws, is caught, and the failure lands in the status line.
    Reset (US-022) is the escape hatch.
  - `indexedDB.deleteDatabase` from the devtools/Playwright console
    fires `onblocked` while the page holds an open connection --
    `createMemberStore` opens one per operation and never closes them.
    Navigate to `about:blank` first, then delete, then navigate back.

