**Learnings for future iterations:**
- **Never call `navigator.clipboard.readText()` through the Playwright
  MCP.** It blocks on a permission prompt that nothing in this harness
  can answer, and the tool call hung for the full 1800s idle timeout --
  which also killed the Vite server and is almost certainly what
  stalled this story the first time. Verify a copy control the safe
  way instead: wrap `navigator.clipboard.writeText` with a
  pass-through recorder to capture the exact argument, treat the
  rendered `Copied` badge as proof the real write resolved, and if a
  true round trip is wanted, focus a throwaway input and send
  `ControlOrMeta+v` via `browser_press_key`. A native paste keystroke
  needs no permission and works.
- Backoff is measurable from the page without touching the client:
  replace `globalThis.WebSocket` with a subclass that pushes
  `Date.now()` in its constructor before calling `super`. The client
  calls `new WebSocket(...)` at connect time, so it picks up the
  subclass, and the gaps between marks are the delays. This is how the
  1012/2004/4005/8006/16006/30009 series above was measured rather
  than eyeballed.
- To stop only the Worker while leaving Vite up, do not use the
  repo-wide `pkill -f ".../node_modules"` from the standing rules --
  that kills Vite and esbuild too. Narrow it to the two Cloudflare
  paths, which are still repo-scoped and so still safe for other
  checkouts:
  `pkill -f "/Users/nick/code/webcrypto-mls/node_modules/@cloudflare/workerd"`
  and `pkill -f "/Users/nick/code/webcrypto-mls/node_modules/wrangler"`.
- A `role="status"` element is not unique on this page -- the `Copied`
  badge is one too, and it comes first in the DOM. Reading
  `document.querySelector('[role="status"]').textContent` to check the
  status line silently returns the badge instead. Read the signal, or
  take a snapshot.
- Verifying page internals needs a handle on them, and the honest way
  is a `TEMPORARY`-marked global removed before the commit. Expose the
  delivery client as well as the state: sending a duplicate `create`
  on the page's *own* socket is what proves the page surfaces the
  refusal, where a throwaway socket only proves the room sends it.
  Gate the commit on `grep -rn "TEMPORARY|__state|__delivery"` finding
  nothing and on `git diff` of the touched file being empty.
- Where a stalled session left work behind, read the diff before
  re-doing anything. This one held a real fix plus a test, and redoing
  the browser pass from scratch would have hidden that the first-open
  case of the same defect was still live.
