   reconnect is pending the socket has already gone, so no second close
   event was coming and nothing ever wrote `'closed'`. The existing
   "closing cancels a pending reconnect" test asserted only that no new
   socket opened, never what the page said afterwards. `close()` now
   writes the terminal state itself. (Confirmed failing: expected
   "closed", actual "reconnecting".)

2. `connect()` did not cancel a scheduled reconnect. A manual connect
   during the backoff window left the timer running; it fired later,
   closed the perfectly good socket it found and opened a third. New
   `cancelReconnect()` is called from both `connect()` and `close()`.
   (Confirmed failing: a third socket appeared and `connection` fell
   back to "connecting".)

3. `entry-queue`'s drain did not contain a throw from `onError`.
   `push()` starts `drain()` and never awaits it, so the throw was an
   unhandled rejection that left `draining` set for the life of the
   page -- after which every push started no drain and was swallowed in
   silence. `onError` writes signals, so it can throw. Now wrapped;
   a handler that fails is treated as `'stop'`. (Confirmed failing:
   the unhandled rejection crashed the node runner outright.)

