## 2026-07-29 - US-010
Phase 7 Tasks 1 and 2: membership derived from the client's own ratchet
tree, plus its unit tests. One subcomponent, one commit.

`example-realistic-demo/client/membership.ts` exports `Member`,
`membersFromTree` and `leafIndexOf`, per phase_07.md Task 1. The list
comes from the tree and nothing else -- the room never parses a commit,
so it cannot know the roster, and the `roster` message is left to supply
only the connected marks. `identity` is the base64url of the leaf's
`signaturePublicKey`, which is what the room already uses on the wire,
so the two lists join without the room being trusted for membership.

`test/example-realistic-demo/membership.ts` holds 27 assertions across
11 tests, registered in `test/index.ts`. The fixtures are hand-built
`RatchetTree` arrays: no group, no crypto, no async, which is the whole
reason the derivation was extracted.

Tests were written before the module and failed at bundle time on the
unresolved `client/membership.js` import.

Seven mutations run, seven killed, via a temporary node harness that
copies the file aside, confirms each replacement landed with `diff -q`,
rebuilds the targeted bundle and restores by file copy:

1. leaf index becomes the node index (7 failures)
2. leaf index renumbers to its position in the list (3)
3. identity read from the credential instead of the signature key (8)
4. blanked leaves not skipped (5)
5. parent nodes not skipped (2)
6. `leafIndexOf` returning 0 rather than null when absent (4)
7. a non-basic credential decoded as basic anyway (7)

Mutation 5 SURVIVED on the first run, and that was the useful finding of
the session. Every fixture put its parent nodes at odd indices, so the
even-index stride skipped them and the `nodeType !== 'leaf'` guard never
ran -- the test named "parent nodes are not members" was passing for a
reason unrelated to the line it claimed to cover. Fixed by adding a
fixture with a parent at an even node index, which is malformed on
purpose: that is exactly the input the guard exists for.

One deviation from the plan, deliberate. Task 1's code writes
`leafIndex: node / 2`; this uses `nodeToLeafIndex(toNodeIndex(node))`
from `src/treemath.js`, because the Codebase Patterns section says tree
index math is always reused rather than re-derived. Behavior is
identical; `nodeToLeafIndex` additionally throws on an odd index, which
this loop cannot produce.

Files changed: `example-realistic-demo/client/membership.ts` (new),
`test/example-realistic-demo/membership.ts` (new), `test/index.ts`,
`example-realistic-demo/AGENTS.md`, `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.

Gates: lint exit 0. Root typecheck exit 2 with one error line, the known
`test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts` TS2769; `--listFiles` confirms
`client/membership.ts` is in that program. Worker typecheck exit 0 with
zero error lines. Full suite 47241 passing, exit 0, up from 47214.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - A guard can be unreachable given the shape of every fixture, and
    the test named after it still passes. Mutation 5 is the cheapest
    possible demonstration: skipping parents was done by the loop's
    stride, not by the guard. When a mutation survives, ask whether the
    line is redundant or the fixtures never reach it -- those need
    opposite fixes, and only the second one is a test defect.
  - A defensive guard against malformed input needs a malformed fixture
    to be load bearing. Building only well-formed trees leaves the
    guard untested by construction.
  - `RatchetTree` and `Node` are not both exported from
    `src/index.js` -- `index.js` re-exports the `RatchetTree` type only,
    so a test needing `Node` imports from `src/ratchet-tree.js`.
    `src/treemath.js` is likewise not on the package entry.
  - A `LeafNode` fixture needs `hpkePublicKey`, `signaturePublicKey`,
    `credential`, `capabilities`, `leafNodeSource`, `extensions` and
    `signature`. `leafNodeSource: 'update'` is the cheapest variant --
    `'commit'` also demands `parentHash` and `'key_package'` a
    `lifetime`.
