    is the Codebase Patterns rule about absence-only tests in a second
    form: not "the test asserts only an absence" but "the fixture cannot
    tell the two behaviours apart". An application entry the client *can*
    decrypt leaves `state.group` alone whether or not the guard runs,
    because `processMessage` returns an application message without a
    `newState`. The fixture that separates them is the one the client
    cannot decrypt.
  - When picking a fixture to kill a guard, ask what the code would do
    with the input if the guard were gone. If the answer is "the same
    thing", the fixture is wrong, not the guard.
  - The liveness display deserves its own browser observation. Every
    happy-path check has all members connected, so `Away` and
    "still listed" are unexercised by construction -- and marking a
    disconnected member as absent from the group is exactly the defect
    the disclosure exists to deny. Close one tab and read the other.
  - Three tabs are enough for this story. Removal needs the removed
    client's own page open to show its state, and the Away check needs a
    member who is not the one being removed, so they cannot be the same
    person.
