  `Persistence` and outside the view switch -- what the server is
  trusted for is as true on the setup form as in a room.
- Fixed a disclosure that US-018 made false: `views/waiting.ts` told
  every joiner to keep the tab open because the invitation key lived
  only in the page. With persistence on that is no longer true, so
  `Waiting` takes a `persist` prop and says the matching thing. The
  file's own docblock had already named this as owed.
- `client/style.css`: `.explainer` and the `.gone-disclosure`, existing
  variables only, nothing outside those two blocks touched.
- Tests: 15 new assertions in `test/example-realistic-demo/views.ts`,
  one new test in `test/example/how-to-use.ts`. Written first and
  watched fail (the two new modules would not resolve).
- Nine mutations, nine killed. Eight against the two new views and
  `waiting.ts`; the ninth -- `HowToUse` ignoring its `steps` prop --
  survived the realistic bundle, because the card renders as an
  unexpanded vnode there and only its props can be asserted. The test
  added to `test/example/how-to-use.ts` kills it.
- Browser: new `scripts/verify-phase8-gone.mjs`, two Chromium profiles,
  10/10. A well-formed id with no room behind it renders the gone view
  and nothing else; the disclosure names both cases; the button lands on
  the setup form with `/` in the URL; the explainer's three parts are on
  the setup view and on the room view; no expiry is claimed before a
  room gives one, and a real room's `data-expires` equals what
  `/api/room/:id` reports; and the waiting disclosure changes with the
  persist toggle, live.
- Gates: lint 0, root typecheck one known key-schedule error and no
  others, worker typecheck 0, `build:realistic` 0.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - A component rendered inside another is an *unexpanded vnode* in the
    node suite -- `Explainer` holds `HowToUse` as a node with props, and
    nothing below it exists to assert on. So a mutation inside the child
    cannot be killed from the parent's test file, however thorough that
    file is. Test the props at the boundary in the parent and the
    rendering in the child's own file; a prop the child ignores is
    invisible to both otherwise.
  - A boolean that drives copy needs two assertions, not one. Marking
    the state in a `data-` attribute and *also* switching the sentence
    gives two independent places to get it wrong, and a test that
    checks only the attribute passes against a page that shows the
    wrong sentence with the right mark on it -- which is worse than no
    flag. Assert that the two renderings differ, without asserting what
    either says: `JSON.stringify(childrenOf(a)) !== ...(b)` keeps the
    rule against testing HTML text and still kills the mutation.
  - Chromium logs every 404 response to the console, so a check that
    asserts "no console errors" fails on a page whose whole purpose is
    to handle a 404. Do not filter the message blind -- it would hide a
    missing asset. Record 404 *responses* off `page.on('response')`,
    filter the console line, and assert separately that every 404 URL
    was the one expected and that the expected one actually happened.

