waiting on it -- every check Step 12 would repeat passes against
`localhost`. That checklist box is the only one left unticked. US-025
is the natural place for the owner to decide.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - Two of Task 6's steps do not describe behaviour this design has, and
    saying so is the work rather than forcing a pass. Step 5 asks for a
    client's own past messages to render from the local record *after a
    reload*; persistence stores the cursor and not the log, so a
    restored client resumes above everything it saw and is never re-sent
    it -- there is no history on the page at all, not even a
    placeholder. The criterion is real, but the place it is observable
    is the room's echo of a live send, which `verify-phase8-chat.mjs`
    already covers. What this harness can add there is the negative
    half: a client's own message must never render as a decrypt
    failure.
  - Step 6 asks for a collapsed run of undecryptable entries by driving
    more than four commits while a member is away. It does not happen,
    and the reason is structural: a returning client replays commits and
    messages in log order, so it applies each commit before the messages
    that follow it and never crosses the `retainKeysForEpochs` window.
    Six commits and six messages away produced zero undecryptable
    entries and an epoch that caught up exactly. Assert the rule that
    survives either outcome -- no two undecryptable placeholders
    adjacent -- and report the count as a note.
  - A "the other database is still there" check must read its records,
    not its name. `indexedDB.deleteDatabase` followed by any reopen
    leaves the name in `indexedDB.databases()` with an empty store, so a
    check on names alone passes against the exact failure the criterion
    is about. `otherDemoKeys` reads `getAllKeys` for this reason, and
    the mutation that deletes all three databases only dies because of
    it.
  - Seed those other databases with the store name the real demos use
    (`members`, from `STORE_NAME` in `example-shared/persistence-storage.ts`).
    A hand-seeded store under a different name would still prove
    isolation, but it would stop proving it the day the demos' schema
    matters.
  - Before closing a tab to make a client "go away", turn persistence
    on. Since Phase 8 that is what makes it the same client returning;
    with the toggle off it is a stranger with a new signature key, and
    every replay check would be measuring a fresh join.
  - A throwaway script that imports `playwright` has to live inside the
    repository. Run from `/tmp` it cannot resolve the package, and the
    error looks like a missing dependency rather than a path problem.
  - `build-pages` wipes and rebuilds `public/`, which is gitignored, so
    it is safe to run as a gate. To check the built output rather than
    the dev server, serve `public/` over a tiny static server that
    strips the `/webcrypto-mls` base -- the build sets that base and the
    pages 404 without it.
