## 2026-07-29 - US-021

One review of the whole of Phase 8, US-016 through US-020. Seven issues
found, all fixed here, each test written against the unfixed code and
watched fail first.

The review itself was already in the working tree when this session
started, from earlier attempts that ran out of room before committing:
seventeen modified files, three new ones, nothing logged and `passes`
still false. Per the log's own rule that an entry here is a claim about
the tree rather than a record of it, it was re-verified rather than
trusted -- every gate re-run from scratch and every mutation re-killed --
and then finished. That, not a fresh review, is what this session did.

- **Two writers of `state.group` overlapping (Important).**
  `chat.ts`, `approvals.ts` and `apply-entry.ts` each read the group,
  await an MLS call and assign what it returned. Each serialised itself
  -- a promise chain in two of them, the serial drain in
  `entry-queue.ts` -- and a chain orders its own module's work against
  nothing else. Either lost update is bad and they are bad differently:
  a commit lost leaves this client at an epoch nobody else is at, after
  the room was already told about the new one; a send lost makes a spent
  generation live again, so the next message encrypts a second plaintext
  under the same key and nonce. New `client/group-lock.ts`, built once
  in `index.ts` and passed to all three, with the read of the group
  moved *inside* the job.
- **The echo was matched positionally (Important).** `recordOwn` took
  its plaintext off the front of a FIFO. `send()` returning true only
  means the socket was open, so a frame handed to a socket that dies
  before it flushes is discarded with nothing telling the page -- and
  the next echo then files the lost message's words under a real
  message, for the rest of the session. `state.outbound` holds
  `{ payload, text }` now and the match is on the ciphertext the room
  stores verbatim.
- **A restore did not derive `state.removed` (Important).** The removal
  is in the stored `ClientState`; the signal the page reads is not. A
  removed client came back as a member of a group it is not in, and the
  first replayed entry past its removal threw in `processEntry` and read
  as a fatal commit. Derived in `restore`, and `restoreSession` opens no
  socket for one -- a `hello` would put it back on the roster the
  removal took it off.
- **`storedRoomIsWanted` could not tell two asks apart (Important).**
  As a `string|null` the bare origin and an id that cannot name a room
  both arrived as null, so a mistyped invitation drew the gone view and
  then had it replaced, by this page, with whatever room the browser had
  a session for -- address bar included. It takes the whole `Route` now.
- **`session.watch()` was skipped when the restore threw (Minor).**
  `restore` turns the preference back on before it can fail, so the page
  showed persistence as on and saved nothing for the rest of the
  session. The call is in a `finally`.
- **Two disclosures said less than the truth (Minor).** The persistence
  control did not say the creator token is among what it writes, so
  anything with access to this browser's storage can act as the room's
  creator; and it did not say the messages are not kept, which is the
  one thing "remember this session" is read as promising. Both said now.
- **A comment that named the wrong key (Minor).** `timeline.ts` had
  `names` documented as seq -> name; it is wire identity -> name.

Files changed: `client/group-lock.ts` (new), `client/apply-entry.ts`,
`client/approvals.ts`, `client/chat.ts`, `client/index.ts`,
`client/session.ts`, `client/state.ts`, `client/timeline.ts`,
`client/views/persistence.ts`, `client/views/room.ts`,
`example-realistic-demo/AGENTS.md`; tests
`test/example-realistic-demo/group-lock.ts` and `async-helpers.ts`
(both new), `apply-entry.ts`, `approvals.ts`, `chat.ts`, `session.ts`,
`views.ts`, `test/index.ts`; `specs/prd.json` and this log.

Twenty-one mutations run, twenty-one killed, against a baseline of 2259
assertions in the fast bundle. Twelve cover this session's fixes: each
of the three modules not taking the lock, the lock running jobs
immediately, a rejected job poisoning the queue, the echo matched
positionally, the payload not kept beside the plaintext, both polarities
of the derived `removed`, and both polarities of the route rule. Nine
more re-confirm that the US-016 and US-017 tests can fail at all --
the placeholder off-by-one, the join boundary, the run-of-misses rules,
the unnamed sender, and four in `persistence-storage.ts` including the
session object store never being created.

Gates: `npm test` 47676 passing, exit 0, up from 47652. `npm run lint`
exit 0. Root typecheck reports one error line, the known
`test/test-vectors/key-schedule.ts:73` TS2769; the Worker typecheck
exits 0 with no error lines. `build:realistic` exit 0. `probe.mjs` 20/20,
exit 0, against a live `worker:dev`, since the fixes reach the wire
format the room echoes.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - The three writers really are three. `grep` for assignments to
    `state.group.value` finds nine sites; the other six are not
    read-modify-writes and do not need the lock. `session.ts` restores,
    `views/setup.ts` creates, and `index.ts` writes the joined group on
    `welcome-you` -- and that last one is ordered by a different
    mechanism, `joinPending` in `connection.ts` holding the `log` batch
    until `onControl` settles, with `applyEntry` a no-op while the group
    is null. Before adding a lock, check whether an existing mechanism
    already orders the pair.
  - A test for a lock has to assert the negative half in the window
    where it is true, which means the test decides when the window
    closes. `deferred()` plus a 200ms `longEnoughToHaveFinished()` in
    `test/example-realistic-demo/async-helpers.ts` is the shape: hold
    the lock, start the thing under test, assert nothing happened,
    release, assert it happened in full. Asserting only the final state
    passes against no lock at all.
  - A mutation harness earns its keep the second time it runs. The
    twelve kills this session re-ran in about ninety seconds from a
    `[file, name, from, to]` list, which is what made re-verifying an
    untrusted handoff cheap enough to do rather than skip. Write the
    list to a file as you go, not the mutations by hand.
  - When a story is found already attempted in the tree, the failure
    mode is starting it again. Three sessions re-reviewed Phase 8 from
    the top and each ran out of room before the commit. Read the diff
    first: if the work is real, verifying it and doing the durable steps
    -- log entry, `passes` flip, `git add` -- costs a fraction of
    redoing it, and an uncommitted fix helps nobody.
