- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - US-022's own last learning was that a count in prose goes stale
    first. It went stale one commit later, in the file that recorded it.
    A count is not worth writing at all unless something fails when it
    is wrong -- and nothing here does. This is the second time the
    `scripts/` count has been wrong in that file.
  - Writing a coverage matrix is worth it for what it makes you check,
    not for the table. Every row was verified by grepping the exact
    `test('...')` title out of the file, and three of the titles I was
    about to write from memory did not exist verbatim. A matrix full of
    plausible test names is worse than no matrix, because it is read as
    proof.
  - Rank the evidence, do not just list it. Four kinds -- Node, probe,
    browser, recorded -- and only the first runs on every change. A
    criterion whose only evidence is a browser step done once in a
    previous story is covered in the past tense; that is what checks 21,
    22 and 23 were added to fix, and saying so in the table is what made
    the gap visible.
