## 2026-08-03 21:20 - US-024

Wrote `docs/test-plans/2026-07-28-realistic-demo.md`, the human test
plan. Docs only; nothing under `src/`, `example-realistic-demo/` or
`test/` changed, so there is no test to write first. What a test would
have to assert here is that a person reading the plan can tell pass from
fail, which is not a machine-checkable property -- and the global rule
forbids writing tests for docs. The checks that do apply were run: both
typechecks, lint, the full suite, and a line-width and character sweep
over the new file.

**Verifying the coverage claim rather than trusting it.** The story's
second criterion is that each requirement has an automated test where
one is possible. `ac-coverage.md` from US-023 asserts that mapping, so
the work here was checking its 93 evidence claims against the tree
instead of re-deriving them: every quoted Node test title matched in
`test/example-realistic-demo/`, every quoted browser check title in
`example-realistic-demo/scripts/verify-phase*.mjs`, and every probe
number against the 23 `await check(n, ...)` calls in `probe.mjs`. All
resolved. Two apparent misses were my matcher, not the document: an
escaped apostrophe in `applyEntry - this client\'s own message ...` and
in `delete takes only this demo\'s database`. The three criteria without
standing automated coverage (AC8.2's handler, AC1.2's deployed half,
AC6.5 across a reload) are the ones the plan is built around.

**The plan's shape.** Standing checks first, with their commands and
expected numbers, because hand-testing a tree that fails its own suite
proves nothing. Then Part 1, the two-profile join by hand; Part 2, the
persistence toggle; Part 3, expiry and the alarm handler, which is the
criterion with no standing check at all; Part 4, reading the page's
disclosures, which no test asserts because the house rule forbids
asserting on HTML text; Part 5, the deployed origin, gated on the
owner's approval. A result sheet and a "things that look like failures
and are not" section close it.

Files changed: `docs/test-plans/2026-07-28-realistic-demo.md` (new),
`progress.log`, `specs/prd.json`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - A human test plan has to name its *expected* result even where that
    result is a limitation. Step 2.7 says a client's own messages come
    back as placeholders after a reload and that this is a pass, with
    the failure being the opposite -- if they came back as text,
    something is storing plaintexts. Written the obvious way round, the
    step would have a tester report a design decision as a bug on every
    run, and eventually stop running it.
  - Part 3 is the only place a criterion is verified by *editing the
    Worker*, so the plan makes removing the edit a numbered step with
    its own expected result (`grep` prints nothing, `git diff` empty).
    A temporary harness with no removal step in the checklist is a
    harness that gets committed.
  - Every concrete number and name in a plan like this is a claim to
    check against the code: the room id is 10 characters
    (`ROOM_ID_LENGTH`), the group id is 32 random bytes, the private
    signature key is `privateKeys.signaturePrivateKey`, and the other
    two demos' databases are `mls-persistence-demo` and
    `mls-multi-device-demo`. Three of those four were wrong in the first
    draft. A plan that misnames what to look at fails in the tester's
    hands and reads as a product defect.
  - The manual run uses the Worker origin (`npm run build:realistic`
    then `npm run worker:dev`, port 8787), not Vite's 1234. That way the
    page and the socket share one origin and the SPA fallback under test
    is the Worker's own `not_found_handling`, and there is no HMR socket
    to confuse with the room's.

